1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ASAHEL BURNHAM
2 CONT
2 CONT Asahel Burnham served in the American Revolution in Captain Samuel Ransom's Wyoming Valley Company, then in Captain Simon Spaulding's Independent Company. These companies were recruited in what was then known as "Westmoreland Coun
2 CONC ty, Connecticut," in the valley of the Susquehanna River around Wilkes-Barre in what is now Pennsylvania. Wyoming Valley was settled by people from Connecticut under that colony's royal charter and the sponsorship of the Connectic
2 CONC ut Susquehanna Land Co. Their land claims were disputed by descendants of William Penn and the area was transferred to Pennsylvania after the war. The book "Western Gateway" by Ray Herrington is a fictionalized version of his life
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT "[The companies] joined Washington's army in New Jersey about Jan. 1, '77, and engaged in the 'affairs' of Millstone River and Bound Brook in New Jersey as well as in the battles of Brandywine and Germantown in Pennsylvania. A det
2 CONC achment of Ransom's Co. under Lieut. Spalding formed part of the garrison at Ft. Mifflin mentioned on p. 129. The companies wintered at Valley Forge, '77-78, with the Connecticut Regiments. In the spring of '78 rumors of a threate
2 CONC ned Indian attack upon Wyoming reached camp and Capts. Durkee and Ransom resigned their commands to return to assist in the protection of their homes. A few of the men also returned, but the companies as such were retained with th
2 CONC e army. Under Act of Congress, June 23, '78, the two companies were consolidated into one and placed under the command of Lieut. Spalding, lately promoted Captain. In the latter part of June he was ordered to Wyoming but failed to
2 CONC  arrive there before the Indian attack and massacre of July 3, '78 (well known in American history), occurred. In that attack settlers and settlements alike were swept away; and among many others who lost their lives were Capts. D
2 CONC urkee and Ransom. Spalding's Co. soon re-entered the Valley, built a new fort, and remained there until the fall of '80. In the meantime, in the summer of '79, it joined Sullivan's expedition against the western Indians [Iroquois]
2 CONC . ... These Wyoming men represented and were paid by Connecticut as part of her Continental contingent...." (from Connecticut in the Revolution, p. 263)
2 CONT
2 CONT In Major General John Sullivan's campaign against the Iroquois, Capt. Spalding's Co served as scouts for the 3rd Brigade under Brigadier General Edward Hand, and Lieut. John Jenkins kept a journal of their actions and movements. T
2 CONC he three line regiments and other units in Hand's Brigade were all from Pennsylvania. Several journals from the campaign were published in 1887 after a centennial celebration. (Cook, Frederick [Conover, George S., compiler]. Journ
2 CONC als of the Military Expedition of Major General John Sullivan against the Six Nations of Indians in 1779 with Records of the Centennial Celebrations. Auburn, NY, Knapp, Peck & Thomson, Printers, 1887.)
2 CONT
2 CONT The list of Privates in Captain Spalding's Co says that Asahel Burnham, residence "Westmorland", enlisted Jan. 1, '77, and was one of several men who were paid to Jan. 1, '80, and probably continued in service until Nov., '80. (Co
2 CONC nnecticut in the Revolution, p. 266)
2 CONT
2 CONT Some data about his family is from Walter Burnham and the 1881 family history.
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughters of the American Revolution Patriot Index, "b 9- -1737 CT d 8-13-1820 NY m Hannah Wadsworth Pvt CT PNSR."
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogical Abstracts of Revolutionary War Pension Files says, "S44712, BLW #447-100, CT Line, appl 1 Jul 1819 Tioga Cty NY aged 81 in Sep 1819 a res of Chemung NY, enl in Wyoming PA, sol was b in 1737 & d 3 Aug 1820." (White, Vi
2 CONC rgil D. Genealogical Abstracts of Revolutionary War Pension Files. Waynesboro, TN, The National Historical Publishing Co., 1990)
2 CONT
2 CONT Library of the Chemung Valley History Museum (301 Water Street, Elmira, NY) has a file card for his grave at Wells Cemetery, Chemung Twp, Chemung Co, NY. Wells cemetery (NOT Wellsburg Cemetery) is a deserted burial site south of t
2 CONC he Chemung River and NY-427. It is "3 miles east of the village of Wellsburg, 2.8 miles west of NY/PA line, on the hillside in a grove of pines, 300 feet south of the highway."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Western Gateway" by Ray Herrington says Asahel and Hannah were married at the East Hartford Meeting House by Rev. Eliphalet Williams. It also says he had reached the age of majority and was executor of his father's estate in 1754
2 CONC , which would place his year of birth at 1733 or earlier. It also says that the first child of Asahel an
