1 NOTE {geni:about_me} One note says of him, "Roger Merrill seemed to be a rover and liked to move.  He wa a farmer in Vermont.  "Aunt" Susanna Marr Jay wrote in March 4, 1925 letter to Effie Louise Fauver Valentine: "I was very small gi
2 CONC rl when I first remember seeing him.  He lived with my mother's mother when he was old and helpless, could not hear or see for many years before he died and lived to be 98 years old.  He was from state of Connecticut.  He served i
2 CONC n the Revolutionary war under Washington and he drew a pension from the United States for many years which he drew on two powder marks on the right side of his nose.  I knew him since I was a little girl until he died and I attend
2 CONC ed his funeral when I was about 17 or 18 years old."- From Leila Hope Valentine Oberto's personal records.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Transcription of Roger Merrill's Revolutionary War Documents Roger is the greatgrandfather of Bill, Don, Bob, Lane, Helen and Jean Merrill. My (Nancy Merrill Sayed's) notes in parentheses.
2 CONT
2 CONT This letter and its contents were made available through Judy Oberto,  daughter-in-law of Leila Oberto (who wrote the 1936 letter discussed on page 7 below).  Thanks, Judy, for this and other primary source documents, letters, pic
2 CONC tures, etc.!
2 CONT
2 CONT Page One:
2 CONT
2 CONT Service:  Conn. (Connecticut) Continental Name:  Roger Merrell or Merrill Number: S. 23.318
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 2:
2 CONT
2 CONT (Page 2 is hard to read on the top; will leave blanks where paper is torn away, and ?? for illegible letters)
2 CONT
2 CONT ?? Al?_______ Roger of Cummy (Connecticut?) in the _______________ who was a Private in the Compa(ny?) by Captain of the Regt(?) by Col. Webb in the C?nnt for 2 years
2 CONT
2 CONT ctf(?) 30939
2 CONT
2 CONT Subscribed on the Roll of Albany at the rate of 80 Dollars 00 Cents per annum to commence on the 4th day of March, 1831 (or 4?)
2 CONT
2 CONT Certficate of Pension found the 7 day of Jary (January) (illegible) 1836(?) sent Hon  Taylor H (illegible)
2 CONT
2 CONT Arrears to the 4th of September 1836 $440.00 Semi-anl. allowance ending 4 Mar '37    40.00
2 CONT                                                             $480.00
2 CONT
2 CONT 			Revolutionary Claim 			Act June 7, 1832 Recorded by D. D. (?) Addison (?) Book A (?) 2, Vol. 4 Page 45
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 3
2 CONT
2 CONT State of New York Oneida County
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 19th day of February 1820 before me the subscriber one of the ??? in and for the said County of Oneida, personally appeared Roger Merrill, aged sixty-four  years and upwards, resident in the town of Trenton in the said Coun
2 CONC ty of Oneida, who being by me first duly sworn according to law, doth on his oath make the following declaration, in  order to obtain the provision made by the late act of Congress, entitled "An act to provide for certain persons 
2 CONC engaged in the  ??? and ??? service of the United States in the revolutionary  war.  That he the ??? Roger Merrell enlisted in the town of New Hartford in the State of Conneticut (sic), in the company  commanded by Captain James W
2 CONC atson in Conl. Samuel B. Webb's Regt. that he enlisted on the 7th day of April A.D. 1777, that he  was attached to the company of Capt. Williams when he joined the Regt. at Peakskill in the State of New York some time in  June fol
2 CONC lowing; that he continued to serve in said Company  and Regt. in the service of the United States untill (sic) the 7th day of April AD 1780 when he was discharged from service in  Morristown in the State of New Jersey; that he was
2 CONC  not in any  battle during said service, and that he is in reduced circumstances and stands in need of the assistance of his Country for a support, that his sole dependance (sic) now is own hard labor for support; and that he has 
2 CONC no other ??? now in his power (?) beyond what is now offered, of his said service.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sworn to and declared before me                			Roger Merrell (his signature) the day and year first above  written H.H. (??) Hamilton, Judge
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 4
2 CONT
2 CONT State of New York Monroe County
2 CONT
2 CONT On this second day of September  one thousand eight hundred and thirty four personally  appeared in the Mayors (sic) Court of the City of Rochester in said county before Isaac Hills Esquire Recorder of said city holding said court
2 CONC  Roger Merill residing  in Brighton in said county and state aforesaid, aged seventy nine years this 23rd day of August, 1834.  Who being first duly sworn according to Law doth on his oath make the following Declaration, in order 
2 CONC to obtain the  benefit of the provision made by the Act of Congress passed  June 7th 1832.  That he enlisted in the army of the  United States in the year 1777 with Captain James  Watson in Colonel Jacob B. Webb’s in the Contient 
2 CONC (scratched  out and above that written) Continental  Line of Troops and that he ??? under the following offices viz: After servng a short time under Captain James Watson who resigned and went home, he served under Captain John Wil
2 CONC liams who seconded under Captain Watson  In command of the Company he served until he was discharged which took place in 1780, four miles below Morristown, New York.  When he enlisted his regiment was commanded by Col. Samuel B. W
2 CONC ebb who was taken prisoner while on an excursion to Long Island, as he believes this was sometime in the year 1777 and was seconded (?) in the Command of the Regiment by  Lieutenant Colonel Ebenezer Hunting (?) or Huntin of Norwic
2 CONC h Connecticut who continued in command until this applicant was discharged as aforesaid.  This applicant was under  the command of Brigadier General Varnum of Rhode Island but does not recall how long he ??? ??? under his command,
2 CONC  or who was next in command under General Varnum. After enlisting which was in New Hartford in Connecticut he joined his regiment which was at Peakskill sometime in this summer of 1777.  From Peakskill he was marched to Rege (?) o
2 CONC f Horseneck (?) and again marched back to Fishkill (?) and there he
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 5
2 CONT
2 CONT was employed a short time as a teamster and went to Miliford (?) in Connecticut after this bagage (?)  and on his return he joined his Reigment at a place called (Henley/Hurley?) above (Edopus?) in New York. From there he was agai
2 CONC n marched to Rege(?) Horseneck (?) in  New York, and from there the Regiment made an excursion in  Long Island and he wasn’t with them, but ???? the same night  he went on and marched to New London in Connecticut  and from there r
2 CONC eturned and went to West Point (?) in  New York and went into another quarter (?) and staid (sic) until the 17th day of June 1778 the anniversary of the Battle of Monmouth, when he was marched to Rhode Island where he was put into
2 CONC  General Varnum’s guard and staid (sic) there until a battle was fought and then the army retreated to Trenton and he went with them, where he remained until in December when he was marched into (western/winter?) (quarters/Quarter
2 CONC s???) at M/W????  While here he went out with a small number of men, volunteers for this purpose, with the design of seizing a British Ship of War then  lying at Rhode Island; but the expedition failed  in consideration of the ice
2 CONC . He remained at (Maine/Varnum’s?)  during the winter, and then marched again to Trenton and (remained?) until November.  From here he was marched through Connecticut to the vicinity of Morristown, New York and here went into wint
2 CONC er quarters where he (remained?) until the 7th day of April, 1780, where he was discharged from the army.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He lived at the time he enlisted in New Hartford, Litchfield County, Connecticut and had been in the Connecticut State Troops one year previous to enlisting in the Regular Army.  He can not remember the number (?) of the Regiment 
2 CONC in which he served nor the name by which it  was known except that of Webb and Hunting or Huntin’s Regiment when he first joined the Regiment. Livingston (?) of  New York was Lieutenant Colonel but resigned soon after.   He recoll
2 CONC ects that some part of the time he was under the
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 6
2 CONT
2 CONT command of General Putnam and (?????) (??) guard?  several times before his (Marker?) and thinks  this was in Peakskill or that vicinity.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	He lost his discharge while a young man which he received (?) from General Hunting or Huntin, and which was given at  what they called the “Huff/Hutt?” four miles below Morristown.   He once had a certificate of the amount of mon
2 CONC ey due  him as a soldier, but it was long since worn out by  carrying it in his pocket.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 	He hereby relinquishes his every claim whatever to a pension or an annuity except the present and he declared that his name is not on the pension roll of any agency in any state.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Sworn to and subscribed the day and  year aforesaid, (his signature Roger Merrell) in open court 
2 CONT
2 CONT The above named Roger Merrell being sworn says that he  has heard the above papers read and knows the content thereof and that he verily believes the same is true (signature of Jasper S or W Gilbert)
2 CONT
2 CONT And the said court do hereby declare the 	opinion that the above-named applicant was a Revolutionary 	soldier and found as he states.
2 CONT
2 CONT 		I, Jasper S. Gilbert of The  Mayor’s Court of the City of Rochester  do certify that the foregoing contains the  original (providings/presidings?) of the court in the matter of the application of Roger Merrell  for a pension.
2 CONT
2 CONT 	In testimony whereof I have hereunto set my hand and seal of  office this second day of September, one thousand eight hundred and thirty-four.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jasper (W/S?) Gilbert
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 7 Not transcribed (as it’s in legible form) but information gathered from this letter states that:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Leila Oberto  10 Blair Avenue Piedmont, California 
2 CONT
2 CONT Before June 20, 1936 (about a hundred years after the passing of Revolutionary Claim Act of 1832—does this have any significance?  Were the records closed before that?  Etc.?) writes to the administrator (ostensibly of the archive
2 CONC s of the Revolutionary War pension office?), asking for information about, and the executive assistant to the administrator writes back on June 20, 1936, concerning:
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger Merril (of Connecticut), a pensioner for service in the Revolutionary War (How did Mrs. Oberto know that he was from Connecticut?  See if I can find from Judy)
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolutionary War records for David and Peter Lasley of Virginia or Pennsylvania
2 CONT
2 CONT Revolutionary War records for Peter, Moses and Andrew Eby of Virginia or Pennsylvania
2 CONT
2 CONT Letter states that there is no information about Lasley or Eby.
2 CONT
2 CONT About Roger Merrell/Merrill (information based on file for pension claim S.23318, for services in the Revolutionary War):
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger Merrell, pensioned as Merrill, was born August 23, 1755 in West Hartford, Connecticut.  His parents are not named. (Where did they get this information? Are there other files that we could access?)
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger Merrell enlisted, date not stated, and served one year in the Connecticut Troops.  Names of officers not stated.  While a resident of New Hartford, Litchfield County, Connecticut, he enlisted on April 7, 1777.  (21 years old
2 CONC )
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger Merrell served in Captain Hart’s, James Watson’s and Samuel Williams’ Companies, Colonel Samuel B. Webb’s, Lieutenant Colonel Livingston’s, and Lieutenant Colonel Ebenezer Huntingdon’s Continental Regiment.  (see if we can f
2 CONC ind out more about them)
2 CONT
2 CONT He was discharged April 7, 1780. (Where was he from 1780 to 1797 when he first appeared in the Percy County tax rolls, Northumberland County, Ontario/Cramahe, Newcastle District, Upper Canada).
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger Merrell was allowed pension on his application executed February 19, 1820, while a resident of Trenton, Oneida County, New York.  He also lived in Whitestown, Oneida County, New York, and in Pompay (?), Onondaga County, New 
2 CONC York. In 1834, he was living in Brighton, Monroe County, New York.  In 1835, he was living in Rochester, New York. (search for any census/other information on him in these other places)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1836, he was living in Cramahe, Newcastle District, Upper Canada.  (He was also on the tax rolls of the same, Cramahe, Percy Township from 1797 onwards, and most of his children were born in Canada.  In the land acquisition doc
2 CONC uments in Canada, it stipulated that the people acquiring the land had to live on it—there was quite a fuss about someone who’d stated that people living there were actually NOT there.  And I wonder if the pension people knew he’d
2 CONC  been living in Canada from 1797 upwards, if that’d have made a difference in the pension disposition?)
2 CONT
2 CONT Stated that Roger Merrell reared twelve children, but names of children and wife are not mentioned.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1836, Roger’s brother, Simeon, lived in Butler, Wayne County, New York.  (is this in another pension application?  If so, find it)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1836, Jerusha Merrell made an affidavit in Madison County, stating that during the most of the soldier’s service, she’d lived in the home of his father, but didn’t state either his or his father’s name, nor her relationship to 
2 CONC either one. (I think Jerusha was Roger’s sister-in-law, maybe Simeon’s wife?  Check this out some more…)
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed by A. D. Hiller, executive assistant to the Administrator, June 20, 1836.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Enlisted in the Revolutionary War on April 7, 1777.  He served in Captains Hart's, James Watson's and Samuel Williams' Companies, Colonel Samuel B. Webb's, Lieutenant Colonel Livingston's and Lieutenant Colonel Ebenezer Huntington
2 CONC 's Continental Regiment and was discharged April 7, 1780.  He was granted a military pension on Febuary 19, 1820 while a resident of Oneida County, New York.  In 1836 he was living in Cramahe Township, New Castle district, Upper C
2 CONC anada, which is now called Cramahe Township, Northumberland County, Ontario Province, Canada.
