2 CONT PICTURES:
2 CONT "Photo of Samuel Terry":
2 CONT Picture of Samuel Terry (B-1/24/1774, D-5/4/1853).  Date of photo is unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to, Samuel Terry's identification # is AFN: CKFS-L9
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Terry of Bristol purchased the house that was to become known as the Terry Homestead, with 16 acres of land and "other buildings" from Col. Thomas Botsford in 1828.  Botsford's granddaughter, Ruth Lewis Hubbell married Samu
2 CONC el's son Ralph Ensign, and thus the two families are linked in blood as well as in real estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Terry was born in East Windsor, CT (now South Windsor) on January 24, 1774.  In 1818, Samuel and his family moved from East Windsor to Plymouth, CT.  Ten years later, Samuel relocated to Bristol, where he died on May 4, 185
2 CONC 3.  Through his wife, Esther (Gillett) Terry, Samuel sired ten children: Samuel Steele Terry (1798-1867), William Terry (1800-1822), Esther G. Terry (b. 1801), Ralph Ensign Terry (1804-1892), John Burnham Terry (1806-1870), Theodo
2 CONC re E. Terry (1808-1881), Hannah  Morton (Terry) Smith (1810-1841), Edward Terry (1812-1866), Milo Terry (1814-1870) and Franklin Kellogg Terry (1815-1855).  With the publication of Stephen Terry's book, "Notes of Terry Families" i
2 CONC n 1887, as well as additional genealogical research that has been conducted over the years, much of the history of the Terry family has been well documented.  However, even as late as 1996, there were still major gaps in what was 
2 CONC known about Samuel Terry's children.  With recent research, some of these gaps  have finally been filled in. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Clockmaking and Other Enterprises of Samuel Terry's Descendants
2 CONT It is well known that Samuel Terry (1774-1853) was founder of a family eight sons, seven of whom were clever and able clockmakers.  Samuel's and his sons have often been overshadowed by his older brother Eli and Eli's sons.  But, 
2 CONC the truth is that Samuel and his sons made and marketed tens of thousands of quality wood and brass clocks.  Samuel Terry's seven clockmaking sons were:
2 CONT Samuel Steele Terry (1798-1867), the eldest son, spent much his career as a foreman for Phelps, Dodge & Company's clockmaking enterprises as Ansonia, CT.  Clock movements for the trade were made during his tenure there as well as 
2 CONC complete clocks marketed under the labels of Ansonia Clock Company, Ansonia Brass Company, Ansonia Brass & Battery Company, and the Ansonia Brass & Copper Company.  Samuel's son, William A. Terry is well known for his development 
2 CONC of a calendar clock.
2 CONT Ralph Ensign Terry (1804-1892), the third son, was a partner in the brass clockmaking firms of R. & J. B. Terry, Terry, Downs, Burwell & Co. and Terry, Downs & Co.
2 CONT John Burnham Terry (1806-1870), the fourth son, was a partner in the brass clock manufacturing firm of R. & J.B. Terry before moving to Hartford where he prospered in the dental profession.
2 CONT Theodore Terry (1808-1881), the fifth son, was perhaps the best known of Samuel Terry's clockmaking sons.  He was the primary partner in Terry & Andrews (1841-1850), the Ansonia Clock Company (1850-1854), Terry, Barnum & Company (
2 CONC 1855-1856).  Theodore was also President of the Terryville Manufacturing Company (1852-abt. 1859).  His son Hubbell P. Terry also worked in this firm.  Theodore and his son ran the operation after Theodore's cousin, Silas B. Terry
2 CONC , left the firm in 1854.
2 CONT Samuel Terry's sons William (1800-1822), Edward (1812-1866) and Milo Terry (1814-1870) were lesser known clockmakers, their working careers spent in the employ of other clockmaking firms.
2 CONT One branch of Samuel Terry's descendents, the family of  Ralph E. Terry, remained in Bristol at Terry's homestead and his descendants still maintain the house, presently home of the Bristol Historical Society.  This branch of Terr
2 CONC y's family has also contributed much to the 20th century Bristol industry.
2 CONT Fortunately for horologists, Samuel and his descendents did not easily throw things away.  When Samuel's great granddaughter, Miss Josephine Terry died at the Terry homestead in 1972, some twenty account books and booklets and mor
2 CONC e than 4,000 loose documents and receipts pertaining to the Samuel Terry family clockmaking enterprises were tucked away in the home.  In the attic of the house with its many generations of family accumulation, were clocks, clock 
2 CONC parts and even experimental movements from several generations of clockmakers.
2 CONT Indeed, Samuel Terry better preserved his clockmaking history than Eli Terry for whom no original business records and few personal records survive.  Samuel's descendents have also kept a good record of their later family heritage
2 CONC  and manufacturing history.
2 CONT Below we have selected some writings about and by later generations of Samuel Terry's family, particularly the family of Franklin Ensign Terry, son of Ralph E. Terry and grandson of Samuel Terry.
2 CONT -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT Following are excerpts from reminiscences written in 1934 by Isabelle Webster (Terry) Fletcher (1878-1972) daughter of Franklin Ensign Terry.
2 CONT " Franklin Ensign Terry was born 1845 in this house (the Samuel Terry homestead).  In two years his parents moved ot the little house on Preston Street, Forestville (where the Prestons lived when I was a girl.  Auntie Anna was bor
2 CONC n there and when she was three years old they moved back to this house again, where Papa lived until he died in 1926 on his birthday July 7.  They always said he was a tall and slim lad, at times almost delicate looking, but I nev
2 CONC er heard that he had any serious illness...
2 CONT My great grandfather (Samuel Terry) bought this house in 1828, and they made some alterations just what I do not know as the Botsfords changed it some too.  I think he tore out the big chimney and did away with the old fireplaces 
2 CONC which were in each front room and in the old long dining room.  The chimney went up in the front hall.  After this was taken out the chimney only came up in to the garret and stove pipes had to go through the bedrooms to the attic
2 CONC  chimney.  This had some advantages as they warmed the rooms they passed through.
2 CONT Samuel Terry was a brother of Eli Terry the clock maker.  He worked with his brother at one time and later made clocks in his own factory also.  At one time he made clocks down here near the river in what we called the old red sho
2 CONC p (although I never saw the original building which burned down before I was born).  He made the first clock in the Congregational Church tower [Third Meeting (Church) House built in 1832]  .  Grandpa Terry (Ralph) [was] also down
2 CONC  in what was once the old Andrews shop near the river just off Preston St., Forestville.  At one time Samuel Terry's children lived around here in various houses.  Samuel Terry Jr. lived in the Beach Lumber Company's house - Edwar
2 CONC d in Daly's - John in the Hume place - Theodore in the old Mitchel place with the pillars and one over on Pine St. where the Garey's lived...
2 CONT Papa worked in the old Welch Clock shop which stood near the railroad tracks near the Forestville station.  He turned pinions for a long while and thoroughly understood a clock movement.  Mr. Henry Bancroft and also his father...
2 CONT At the time I was married June 2, 1897 Papa was working in Winsted, [sic] Connecticut in the clock shop.  His father had worked there years before and had been foreman of the movements department.  Emerson (Terry) also worked ther
2 CONC e at the same time as his father at the age of sixteen.  Papa had been out of work here in Forestville for a long time.  There was a great depression...
2 CONT Grandpa Terry could always fix most anything that got out of order and also make new things and he always had the tools to do it.  If he did not have them he could make them.  There have been many tools here in the place that were
2 CONC  homemade.  Papa was the same way, he could do almost anything and was very particular...
2 CONT Papa and Mamma were married Aug. 2, 1870 eight years later Jan. 6, 1878 I came to town...
2 CONT In 1893 March Fred [Fletcher, Isabelle's future husband] came East stopping at the World's Fair Grounds.  The Fair had not  yet opened.  He came to work for his Uncle Samuel Monce who owned  a business conducted in the old shop wh
2 CONC ich stood near the river where the Bell shop formerly stood.  The Bell shop was dismantled last year (1933).  He was  twenty years old...It was not until 1895 or so that I began to keep company with Fred...After graduating I becam
2 CONC e formally engaged and after a year I was married, June 2, 1897, here at home in the South Parlor, the same room in which my Great Grandmother Hubbell had been married...
2 CONT The factory [old shop near the river noted above] had burned down in Bristol and his uncle had moved the business to his own factory in Unionville.  We lived in Unionville three years.  Arthur was born there on April 15, 1898 in t
2 CONC he house on Farmington Ave. owned by Mr. Freeborn Alger, I have pleasant recollections of my home there and I made many friends...
2 CONT In 1900 Mamma was hurt by our horse and that year I came back to Bristol to take care of her eventually we moved here for Fred was dissatisfied in the way his uncle treated him in the business.  He was only getting two dollars a d
2 CONC ay when we were married and he had only one raise after that of twenty-five cents.
2 CONT Fred had been inventing a new glass cutter with interchangeable wheels.  This he offered to his Uncle but got no encouragement in regard to his adopting it.  Fred wanted to go into business for himself and after coming back home t
2 CONC o live Papa got Fred a position in the clock shop in Forestville.  In the fall of 1901 and into 1902 Fred started a business in the upper barn here on the place.  He worked nights here and held his place at the clock shop.  Then h
2 CONC e had to get someone to work here all the time.  These were bad years for him and he had to work hard chores and all made him busy.
2 CONT Papa was working at the Clock Shop too and it was about this time or a little later that Mr. Sessions gave Papa a good position.  Superintendent of the Movement department.  At last Papa was working so many years at the bench (all
2 CONC  at clock work) had a good position and one he was perfectly capable of filling efficiently.  He thoroughly understood clock movements both how to make them and making them run.  He could always fix a clock.
2 CONT He held his position until several years later when he resigned and gave all his time to our own business which had been organized and run under the name "The Fletcher-Terry Co."  It was a long hard pull in those years and Fred ha
2 CONC d to work very hard."
2 CONT
2 CONT See "Remarks" for Samuel Terry's probate records.
2 CONT
2 CONT "'Eli Terry Born in 1772'
2 CONT Two of their children, Eli born in 1772, and Samuel, born in 1774, were destined to become leaders in the clock making industry.
2 CONT Samuel Terry, fifth, the first of his name in Bristol, was born in East Windsor, in 1774, and must have come equipped with the drive and energy that carried him through life, for, at the age of 24, we find him already in business 
2 CONC for himself, in his native town, making harnesses, bridles, leather goods, etc.  His books, dated as early as 1798, are beautifully kept and still easy to read.  They give a day to day account for his business transactions.  All t
2 CONC he old names of East Windsor, from the minister down, are shown as his customers.  He was a sergeant of the First Brigade, Connecticut State Militia, from which he received his honorable discharge in 1811, at the age of 37.
2 CONT Even before going to work for his brother, Eli, the clockmaker Samuel was building and caring for towner clocks.  His records show that he made one for the East Windsor church in 1811, and for the Norfolk church in 1815.  Later, h
2 CONC e made clocks for churches in Litchfield (1830), Peeskill (1830), Bristol (1832), and Waterbury (1845)."
2 CONT
2 CONT "'Samuel and Esther Terry Raise Large Family'
2 CONT As was the custom of the times, Samuel and Esther Terry raised a large family.  One son and one daughter died while the family were still living in East Windsor.  And again as ususal, most of the sons lived nearby and worked on th
2 CONC eir father's many projects.
2 CONT Theodore built the bit shop .---------These men and women of the past remain as firmly entrenched in our history as the New England hills.  They are as eternal as its sky.  It is of them and their inspiring deeds that we think whe
2 CONC n we speak of America as the young and vigorous land that first offered freedom to mankind in return for honest effort, love and loyalty.  If there are modern Americans, so-called, ideals and turned to less worthy paths, the rugge
2 CONC d pioneers appear all the brighter by contrast."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Samuel Terry, like most of the early settlers, was an energetic and influential citizen.  Even before he moved his goods to Bristol, we find, from an old deed, that he purchased from Philo Pierce and Leonard Parsons, a grist mill
2 CONC , will all the priveleges, including one-half the milldam.  Two years later, there is a deed to Samuel Terry by Philo Pierce, for two pieces of land, one fifteen rods, the other of seven and three quarters acres, bounded west on h
2 CONC ighway, north on land of Julius Pierce, south on land of Orren L. Botsford.  About the same time, he bought a sawmill and a shop from Charles Kirk and Philo Pierce." 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Samuel Terry, fifth, the first of his name in Bristol, was born in East Windsor, in 1774, and must have come equipped with the drive and energy that carried him through life, for, at the age of 24, we find him already in buiness 
2 CONC for himself, in his native town, making harnesses, bridles, leather goods, etc.  His books, dated as early as 1798, are beautifully kept and still easy to read.  They give a day to day account of his business transactions.  All th
2 CONC e old names of East Windsor, from the minister down, are shown as his customers.  He was a sergeant of the First Brigade, Connecticut State Militia, from which he received his honorable discharge in 1811, at the age of 37.
2 CONT Even before going to work for his brother, Eli, the clockmaker Samuel was building and caring for tower clocks.  His records show that he made one for the East Windsor church in 1811, and for the Norwalk Church in 1815.  Later, he
2 CONC  made clocks for churches in Litchfield (1830), Peekskill (1830), Bristol (1832) and Waterbury (1845)."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Samuel Terry built three shops on the south side of the Pequabuck River, in Bristol, near the site of the present Rolling Mill dam, and carried on quite extemsively for those days.  He retired in 1834, but he evidently did not lo
2 CONC se sight of the future of Bristol, for, when the railroad was planned, he sold the company a piece of land.  This is shown in the Bristol Land Records.  
2 CONT After Samuel, fifth, retired, the business was carried on by two of his sons, under name of Ralph E. and John B. Terry through 1935 and 1936.  At that time, John withdrew to take up the study of medicine.
2 CONT The business went on under the name of Terry & Andrews from 1842 to 1850."
