1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://georgiainfo.galileo.usg.edu/gahistmarkers/butlerhistmarker.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT DR. ELIZUR AND ESTHER BUTLER
2 CONT
2 CONT MISSIONARIES TO THE CHEROKEE INDIANS 
2 CONT
2 CONT Buried in the grave sixty feet south of this point is Esther
2 CONT
2 CONT Post Butler. Born in Connecticut on September 15, 1795,
2 CONT
2 CONT Post married Dr. Elizur Butler, physician and minister, in October
2 CONT
2 CONT 1820. The Butlers were sent by the American Board of Commissioners
2 CONT
2 CONT for Foreign Missions to Brainerd and then to Creek Path, before
2 CONT
2 CONT arriving at the Haweis Mission, near Rome, in 1826. Mrs. Butler
2 CONT
2 CONT died in 1829 after eight years of service to the Cherokees.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two years later Dr. Butler was arrested for residing in the
2 CONT
2 CONT Cherokee Nation without taking an oath of allegiance to the State
2 CONT
2 CONT of Georgia and obtaining a license from the Governor. Sentenced,
2 CONT
2 CONT with Samuel Worcester, to four years of hard labor in the State
2 CONT
2 CONT penitentiary at Milledgeville, he was pardoned by Governor Wilson
2 CONT
2 CONT Lumpkin in 1833, almost a year after the United States Supreme Court
2 CONT
2 CONT nullified the law under which the missionaries were arrested.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his release, Dr. Butler returned to the Mission at Haweis,
2 CONT
2 CONT but was forced to move the following year. In addition to attending
2 CONT
2 CONT the Cherokees during the Removal, Dr. Butler served the missions at
2 CONT
2 CONT Red Clay, Park Hill, and Fairfield and the Cherokee Female Seminary
2 CONT
2 CONT prior to his death in Arkansas in 1857.
2 CONT
2 CONT 057-14 GEORGIA HISTORICAL COMMISSION 1957
