1 TITL Brigadier General
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Brigadier General Charles Larned'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/73284566/charles_larned
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth:  Dec. 28, 1791 Pittsfield Berkshire County Massachusetts, USA  
2 CONT Death:  Aug. 13, 1834 Detroit Wayne County Michigan, USA 
2 CONT
2 CONT GEN CHARLES LARNED was a man beloved by rich and poor alike. His death was occasioned by exposure in the interests of humanity, furnishing an instance of self-sacrifice seldom exhibited, either in ancient or modern times. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He left ''"a family, a city and a State in mourning."'' Such was the tribute offered to the memory of the subject of this sketch, by the late Hon. Charles C. Trowbridge. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Larned was a native of Pittsfield, Berkshire County, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Simon Larned, his father was ''"High Sheriff''" of Berkshire County when the Colonies declared their independence, and during the war which followed, was Colonel of the 9th regiment American Infantry, and Aid-de-camp to George Was
2 CONC hington. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the close of the Revolutionary war, Simon, for a time, retired from the army, but during the war of 1812 resumed command of his former regiment, and participated in the battles of Flatbush and those on the eastern frontier, whi
2 CONC le Charles, the subject of this sketch and his brother George were on the frontier at Fort Wayne, River Raisin and Detroit. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Larned was a graduate of Williams College, in 1806. Studied law in the office of Henry Clay, and while a student was one with Governor Shelby and Colonel Owen to engage in the organization of a regiment in aid of Gen. Harr
2 CONC ison, then in command of the western frontier. This was the noted Kentucky regiment that was almost destroyed at the River Raisin massacre, and was subsequently merged into the regular army, and participated in the battle of the T
2 CONC hames. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Larned was the custodian of the document signed by General Cass, Colonel Brush, David Cooper and others, eighty in number, in which they agreed to seize and depose Gen. Hull, and thus prevent his contemplated disgraceful s
2 CONC urrender of Detroit. Through some source Hull was apprised of their intentions and frustrated their plan, by sending Cass, Brush and Miller, with their commands, to meet an expected convoy of supplies then on its way from Ohio. An
2 CONC d history informs us that on their way back they learned of the surrender, and the fact that they and their troops were included as prisoners under its terms.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles was eventually appointed Brigadier-General by U.S. President John Quincy Adams.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the close of the war General Larned engaged in the practice of law, and ranked with the most successful lawyers of the northwest. He was Attorney General during the Black Hawk war, and with Governor George B. Porter, conduct
2 CONC ed the negotiations which grew out of it. He was the adviser and friend of General Cass, and through him and  General John R. Williams, the militia organization of Michigan was reduced to a practical system for efficient work. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Larned became distinguished as a criminal lawyer, and was a brilliant speaker. The poor and unfortunate, as well as the young lawyer, always found a sympathizing friend and helper in General Charles Larned. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He had passed through the cholera epidemic of 1832, but on its return in 1834 he became a victim, owing to his indefatigable efforts to relieve and alleviate its terrible distress and fatality to others, at one period going withou
2 CONC t rest for 48 hours. Governor Porter, a co-worker, was also his companion in death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Larned occupied and owned a cottage-residence and office on the south side of Larned street (which takes its name from him) and Woodward avenue. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1813 General Larned married Miss Sylvia Easton Colt, daughter of Captain James and Miriam Colt, of Pittsfield, Massachusetts. She was born at Pittsfield, Massachusetts, in 1796. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Larned died August 13th, 1834, and she died August 24th, 1845, aged 49 years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT General Larned's family at his death consisted of Julia Colt Larned, Jane Hereford Larned, Sylvester Larned, Catherine Jones Larned, Mary Larned and Harriet H. Larned. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Related Larned papers exist in the Clements Library at the University of Michigan dated 1813 May 1, Simon Larned ALS to Charles Larned; Cantonment Greenbush, [New York]. (3 pages) Regarding family news and troop movements; "The tr
2 CONC oops from all Quarters are moving to Buffalo [sic] & Sacket's Harbor…I think however we shall soon have permanent foothold on Canada side of the Lake. The Recruiting service the last winter has been very successful."
