1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * Updated from [http://www.myheritage.com/research/collection-1/myheritage-family-trees?itemId=199151281-3-5164&action=showRecord&indId=externalindividual-f2255214705b7dc549d5d251c920087f&mrid=eb547733374d7056fdaa9
2 CONC a0b40385a28 MyHeritage Family Trees] by [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jan 15 2015, 7:28:46 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John Hollister (1792-1839), born at Marbletown, New York. Son of Elizabeth Newcomb and Isaac Hollister (Isaac was captured by a band of Indians October 15, 1763 in Wyoming Flats, Pennsylvania and lived to tell of his harrowing exp
2 CONC erience and escape. The same day of his capture, the Indians killed Isaac's father, Capt. Timothy Hollister, and Isaac's older brother.) 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Hollister married Lavina Clearwater abt 1817. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a farmer and moved to Tompkins County, NY and owned a farm adjoining his brother, Isaac Hollister's farm. They lived in Caroline, Tompkins County, New York from 1818-1835 where he fathered and raised their large family of 9
2 CONC  children: Isaac, Mary (Blakely), Melissa (VanHyning), Alva, Keziah (Stiles), Sarah Anne (Lockridge), Lovina (VanHyning), Rachel Catherine (Ferre) and Joanna(Patten). 
2 CONT
2 CONT John was a member of the Closed Communion Baptist Church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evidence shows they moved to Portage County, Ohio ca. 1835 and met with the Prophet Joseph Smith, Dec. 1835, at Portage Co. They continued to investigate Mormonism where they eventually joined the LDS Church. They assisted in buil
2 CONC ding the Kirtland Temple and were present at it's dedication, March 27, 1836.
2 CONT
2 CONT After John's conversion, he and his family were driven by persecutions along with the other Saints of the church to eastern Caldwell County, Missouri abt 1836-1838 (their daughter Joanna Hollister Patten writes she was 3 years old
2 CONC  when they fled to Missouri) and arrived at Hauns Mill the day after the Massacre October 30, 1838. They heard the cannon blast. John escaped the mob but would later die as a result of the exposure he received while secreting hims
2 CONC elf in the woods from the mobs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Official records have not been found of John's death but there are several references that say he died in 1839 in Illinois. He probably became ill as did the children, and the family went back to Illinois when the Prophet told the
2 CONC  Saints to get to a place they could winter. John lived until they reached Illinois and he was one of the many Saints who died that spring.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/88862628/john-hollister Find A Grave Memo
2 CONC rial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Oct 6 2019, 18:28:23 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT <p><em style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-size: medium;">Note:</em><span style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-size: medium;">&nbsp;Two of John's daughters married Van Hynings: Melissa married Sylvester Van Hyning; 
2 CONC Lovina married Sylvester's nephew, Jacob. John is the son of Isaac Hollister and Elizabeth Newcomb.</span></p>
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT <p><em style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-size: medium;">Note:</em><span style="font-family: 'Times New Roman'; font-size: medium;">&nbsp;Two of John's daughters married Van Hynings: Melissa married Sylvester Van Hyning; 
2 CONC Lovina married Sylvester's nephew, Jacob. John is the son of Isaac Hollister and Elizabeth Newcomb.</span></p>
