2 CONT
2 CONT "Hannah Morton (Terry) Smith (1810-1841), and Her Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah Morton, the seventh child of Samuel Terry of Bristol married a man named Samuel B. Smith on May 5, 1830.  The union produced three children:  Samuel B. Smith, Jr.;  Adeline M. Smith;  and Wallace Smith.
2 CONT Samuel, Jr. (1833-1854) lived to the age of 21, and he is buried with his mother and a four year old girl named Ellen Elizabeth Smith in Bristol's Old North Cemetery (now known as the "Lewis Street" Cemetery.)  Until now, Ellen El
2 CONC izabeth had been believed to be Samuel's daughter, by a wife named Henrietta.  Her tombstone states that she is the daughter of  "Samuel and Henrietta Smith."  However, as the girl was born in 1848, when Samuel, Jr. was only 15 ye
2 CONC ars old, it seems unlikely that he would have fathered the girl.  A check of the state marriage records indicated that a "Samuel B. Smith of Bristol" married Henrietta Stoughton in the town of East Winsor on April 23, 1845.  (East
2 CONC  Windsor Vital Records, Volume 1, pg. 116.)  As this wedding took place when Samuel, Jr. was 12 years old, and four years after the death of his mother, it would seem that it was Samuel, Sr.  and not Samuel, Jr. who married Henrie
2 CONC tta.  Thus, young Ellen Elizabeth would not have been Hannah's granddaughter, but, instead, her step daughter.  
2 CONT New information has also been uncovered regarding Hannah's second child, Adeline.  The date of Adeline's marriage to Nathan Loomis Birge has been identified as May 19, 1852.  The ceremony was performed by Rev. W. H. Goodrich.  (Br
2 CONC istol Vital Records, Volume 3, Page 61.)  Cemetery records indicate that Adeline's son, John, married a woman named M. Antoinette Root, which had been spelled as the spelling is, in fact, without the "E", Antoinette could be from 
2 CONC the prominent Root family, once from Southington, which later produced the American statesman Elihu Root.  Research has also uncovered the wife of Adeline's grandson, John Kingsley Birge.  A woman named Anna Harlow (Feb. 12, 1887 
2 CONC - Oct. 10, 1925), who is listed as being his wife, is buried in West Cemetery in Bristol.  Unfortunately, John Kingsley's burial place has yet to be discovered.  
2 CONT No new information has been uncovered on Hannah's third child, Wallace Smith.  His birth and death are still unknown.  A young Connecticut man with that name did die in the Civil War Battle of Winchester, Virginia on June 15, 1863
2 CONC .  That Smith served in the 18th Regiment of Company C of the Connecticut Volunteers.  However, his death is listed in the town records of Woodstock, CT (Woodstock Vital Records, Vol. 3, Page 432), and there is no evidence, at thi
2 CONC s point, that any of Hannah's family moved to that Northeast Connecticut town. 
2 CONT Neither the date of death nor the final resting place of Hannah's husband, Samuel, has been found.  Perhaps he is buried with his second wife in another town.
2 CONT Detailed information on Hannah Smith's family is attached. "
2 CONT
2 CONT State tombstone records list the middle name on Hannah's tombstone as Norton, not Morton
2 CONT
2 CONT State records indicate that Hannah's tombstone lists her date of death by May 19, 1841.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to East Winsor Vital Records, "Samuel B. Smith of Bristol married Henrietta Stoughton of East Windsor on April 23, 1845.  The rite was performed by O.  F.  Parker."  (East Windsor Vital Records, Vol. 1, page 116).
2 CONT Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000185360628979label=@S84@
