1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT - According to, , and, William Terry was born on 6/13/1800 and died 12/29/1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT PICTURES:
2 CONT "Gravestone of William Terry, Palisado Cemetery":
2 CONT Gravestone of William Terry in Palisado Cemetery, South Windsor, CT.  Picture taken by Terry Bradley Fletcher on 6/8/1997
2 CONT
2 CONT "William Terry & Cynthia Gillett's Gravestones":
2 CONT Gravestones of William Terry (on the left) (b-6/13/1800, d-12/29/1822) and Cynthia Gillett (on the right) (b-about 1798, d-4/19/1813) in Palisado Cemetery, South Windsor, CT.  Picture taken by Terry Bradley Fletcher on 6/8/1997.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT William Terry was buried next to Cynthia Gillett (b-about 1798, d-4/19/1813).  Cynthia is his first cousin.  The relationship is as follows: William's mother, Esther (Gillett) Terry (b-2/7/1775, d-9/19/1854), had a brother, Almeri
2 CONC n Gillett (b-9/1/1765) who married Eunice (Griswold) Gillett.  Cynthia Gillett is the daughter of Eunice and Almerin.  
2 CONT
2 CONT "Stephen Terry's book tells us that Samuel Terry's second son, William (June 13, 1800-Dec. 29, 1822), was the first to marry, and the first of Samuel's sons to die.  The book also informs us that William married a woman named Nanc
2 CONC y Harris on Dec. 24, 1821.  Until now, nothing else was known about Samuel's second son.  Recently, however, it was discovered that William's marriage to Nancy Harris took place in the First Church of Windsor, CT.  It was also dis
2 CONC covered that William is buried in Palisado Cemetery, on Palisado Avenue in Windsor.  William's tombstone reads, as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT WILLIAM TERRY,
2 CONT BORN
2 CONT June 13, 1800
2 CONT DIED
2 CONT Dec. 29, 1822
2 CONT
2 CONT INFANT SON OF
2 CONT W. & N. Terry, died Nov. 15, 1822
2 CONT
2 CONT This stone is remarkable in that, for the first time, we learn that William Terry is buried with a heretofore unknown son, who died six weeks before he did.  Unfortunately, William's wife, Nancy Harris is still a mystery.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Clockmaking and Other Enterprises of Samuel Terry's Descendants
2 CONT Samuel Terry's sons William (1800-1822), Edward (1812-1866) and Milo Terry (1814-1870) were lesser known clockmakers, their working careers spent in the employ of other clockmaking firms."
2 CONT
2 CONT "[His wedding] was performed by Rev. Henry A. Rowlands (Windsor Vital Records, Vol. 2, Page 265.)  It is interesting that William was married in the town of Windsor, as the 1820 United States Census leads one to conclude that Will
2 CONC iam was apparently living with his father and his siblings in the town of Plymouth a year earlier.  However, the entire Terry family had relocated to Plymouth in 1818 from East Windsor, and perhaps William remained in contact with
2 CONC  a girl from the Windsor/East Windsor area after moving to Plymouth."
