2 CONT - According to andand and, John Burnham Terry died on 3/14/1870.  However,  lists the year of death as 1833.
2 CONT
2 CONT - According to,,,, and, John Burnham Terry was a homeopathic physician and a dentist in Hartford, CT.
2 CONT
2 CONT OCCUPATION:  A partner in the brass clock manufacturing firm of R. & J. B. Terry.  Later a dentist in Hartford.  Dentist in Hartford, CT and Brooklyn, N.Y and was also a homeopathic physician. 
2 CONT
2 CONT BURIAL:  While interred in Spring Grove Cemetery, Hartford, CT, his grave # was #2636
2 CONT
2 CONT OTHER PROPERTIES OWNED:
2 CONT On February 2, 1832, Grantor Henry Tompkins of Bristol executed a warrantee deed to Grantees Samuel S. Terry, Ralph Terry, John B. Terry, Theodore Terry, and Edward Terry all of Bristol for sixty three dollars for land described a
2 CONC s follows:
2 CONT “[A] certain piece of land situate in said Bristol, containing thirty five rods more or less, bounding northeasterly by the River as it passes the house of Philo Pierce, southerly on land of Ashabel Mix and westerly on highway.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  Henry Tompkins to Samuel S. Terry, Ralph Terry, John B. Terry, Theodore Terry, Edward Terry, Vol. 16, Page 311, February 2, 1832, Land Records, Bristol, Connecticut Town Clerk’s Office. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 21, 1836, Grantors Ralph Terry and John B. Terry both of Bristol executed a quitclaim deed to Grantee Samuel Terry of Bristol for seventy five dollars for land described as follows:
2 CONT “[A] certain building standing situate in the 2nd tier of lots in said Bristol and on the south side of the River, between the Factory and Forgeing Shop of the said Samuel Terry.  The said building has been used by us as a finishi
2 CONC ng and baseing shop -- also in and to all our common and undivided right in a certain piece of land situate in said Bristol - Bounding westerly on a highway, south on the land of Ashabel Mix and easterly and northerly on the river
2 CONC  and contains one rood more or less.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  Ralph Terry to John B. Terry, Vol. 17, Page 544, November 21, 1836, Land Records, Bristol, Connecticut Town Clerk’s Office. 
2 CONT
2 CONT PICTURES:
2 CONT Spring Grove Cem. Record Of Terry/Ives Plot - X's mark the area in which the Ishams were previously buried before their removal from the Spring Grove Cemetery, Hartford, CT and reinterment in the Cedar Hill Cemetery, Hartford, CT.
2 CONT
2 CONT JOHN BURNHAM TERRY CHRONOLOGY
2 CONT
2 CONT May 7, 1806  Born, South Windsor, Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT May 18, 1828  Making clock cases. 
2 CONT
2 CONT June 16, 1828  Married Amanda Joanna (Warner) Terry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT January 20, 1830  Birth of his first son, Edwin Morton Terry.
2 CONT
2 CONT June 1, 1831  Death of his son, Edwin Morton Terry.
2 CONT
2 CONT May 14, 1832  Warrantee deed (v. 16, p. 311) filed in Bristol (CT) City Clerk’s Office receiving the following lands from Henry Tompkins:  “A certain piece of land situate in said Bristol, CT containing thirty five rods more or le
2 CONC ss bounding northeasterly by the river as it passes the house of Philo Pierce, southerly on the land of Ashabel Mix, and westerly on the highway.”   
2 CONT
2 CONT June 28, 1832  Birth of his second son, Emerson Mortimer Terry.
2 CONT
2 CONT June 13, 1834  Birth of his twins, Edgar John Terry and Esther Amanda Terry.
2 CONT
2 CONT June 14, 1834  Death of one of his twins, Edgar John Terry.
2 CONT
2 CONT May 13, 1835  Death of his other twin, Esther Amanda Terry.
2 CONT
2 CONT November 21, 1836  Quitclaim deed (v. 17, p. 544) filed in Bristol (CT) City Clerk’s Office transferring the following lands to Samuel Terry:  “A certain building standing situate in the 21st tier of lots in said Bristol, CT and o
2 CONC n the south side of the River, between the Factory and Forging Shop of the said Samuel Terry.  The said building hass been used by us as a finishing and embossing shop -- also in and to all our common and undivided right in a cert
2 CONC ain piece of land situate in said Bristol, CT.  Bounding westerly on a highway, south on the land of Ashabel Mix and easterly and northerly on the River and contains one rod more or less.
2 CONT
2 CONT February 19, 1837  Birth of his second daughter, Mary Jane (Terry) Isham.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1829-1840  Made with his brother, Ralph Terry, 8-day brass weight-driven mantel clocks.
2 CONT
2 CONT March 5, 1846  Birth of his third daughter, Henrietta Amanda (Terry) Babcock.
2 CONT
2 CONT January 22, 1848  Death of his second son, Emerson Mortimer Terry. 
2 CONT
2 CONT November 15, 1856  Married Hanna ?? (Clapp) Burnham/Terry.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1855-56 to 1862-63  Lived in Hartford, Connecticut. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1859  Member, Hartford, Connecticut Homeopathic Physicians’ Society.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1869-1870  Lived and practiced as a “physician” at 95 High Street, Brooklyn, New York.
2 CONT Another John B. Terry was listed in the Brooklyn City Directory in 1867 as a coal dealer at Broadway and 10th, living at 211 84th Street.  This is not believed to be the same John Burnham Terry, son of Samuel Terry of Bristol, Con
2 CONC necticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT March 14, 1870  Died, Brooklyn, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1967  Humes House, south of the Homestead (54 Middle Street, Bristol, CT), where John Burnham Terry and his wife, Amanda, resided, is torn down.
2 CONT
