2 CONT EXCERPTS
2 CONT "John Burnham Terry (1806-1870) in Brooklyn, NY"  by Martin C. Babicz, received by Terry Bradley Fletcher, December 30, 1996.
2 CONT "Samuel's fourth son, John Burnham Terry, was born in South Windsor, CT on May 7, 1806 and he died in Brooklyn, NY.  A previous check of the Hartford, CT city directories revealed that a "Dr. J. B. Terry" lived in Connecticut's ca
2 CONC ptial city from 1855-56 to 1862-63.  Research at the New York State Archives in Albany, NY provided the opportunity to review Brooklyn (NY) city directories to determine when and where he turned up in the "City of Churches."
2 CONT Throughout the 1860's, there are only two people named John B. Terry who appear in the Brooklyn (NY) city directories.  The earlier of the two is a coal dealer at Broadway and 10th, who was housed at 211 84th Street.  This Terry f
2 CONC irst appeared in the Brooklyn( NY) city directory in 1867.  ("Brooklyn City Directory for the year ending May 1, 1867," published by J. Lain & Company, Brooklyn, NY, 1867, page 556).  However, unless John Burnham Terry made a majo
2 CONC r career change from dentist to coal dealer, this person is not the son of Samuel Terry of Bristol, CT.
2 CONT A second "John B. Terry" first appeared in the Brooklyn (NY) city directory in 1869.  This Terry is listed as a "physician" who was housed at 95 High Street.  ("Brooklyn (NY) City Directory for the year ending May 1, 1869, publish
2 CONC ed by J. Lain & Company, Brooklyn (NY), 1869, page 616).  He apparently practiced at his home, as a listing of physicians in the directory bears the same address.  (Ibid., page 769).  This gentleman is probably John Burnham Terry,
2 CONC  the fourth son of Samuel Terry of Bristol, CT.  Interestingly enough, like the Hartford city directories of a decade earlier, he is listed as a physician, rather than as a dentist.
2 CONT John B. Terry, the physician, is also in the 1870 Brooklyn (NY) City Directory.  (Brooklyn City Directory for the year ending May 1, 1870, published by J. Lain & Company, Brooklyn (NY), 1870, page 648.)"
2 CONT
2 CONT William L. Willard has discovered that John Burnham Terry was definitely a homeopathic physician in the mid-1800's in Hartford, Connecticut and Brooklyn, New York.  John Burnham is listed as a member of the 1859 Hartford Homeopath
2 CONC ic Physicians' Society in the 1859 Hartford County Version of the Connecticut Register.  William L. Willard has not yet confirmed that John Burnham Terry was a dentist during that time period although John's first cousin, Stephen,
2 CONC  characterized him as one.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Dr. John Burnham Terry of Hartford, a dentist who shared his office with Dr. Horace Wells, the inventor of laughing gas anesthesia." 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel's fourth son, John Burnham Terry, was born in South Windsor, CT on May 7, 1806 and that he died in Brooklyn, NY on March 14, 1870.  Stephen Terry reports that he was married twice, first to Amanda Joanna Warner who died on 
2 CONC Jan. 1, 1856, and then to Hannah (Clapp) Burnham, the widow of Leander Burnham.  Stephen Terry also reports that John Burnham Terry was a dentist, first in Hartford (CT) and then in Broolyn, NY.  
2 CONT We already knew that John Burnham Terry married Amanda Joanna Warner Terry, the daughter of David and Lois (Sutcliff) Terry on June 1, 1828.  Previously , we knew little of John Burnham's second marriage.  Research into John Burnh
2 CONC am's second marriage uncovered a wedding announcement in the Oct. 18, 1856 issue of the "Hartford Times".  According to the newspaper, Mrs. Hannah C. Burnham, of Hartford, CT married John B. Terry of Hartford, CT in Hartford, CT o
2 CONC n October 15, 1856. The research also uncovered information on Hannah's first marriage.  According to the state marriage records, Hannah Clapp of Hartford, CT was married in New Haven, CT to Leander C. Burnham of New Haven, CT on 
2 CONC October 4, 1839.  The ceremony was performed by Nath(aniel) W. Taylor.
2 CONT There has been interest expressed as to when John Burnham Terry moved from Bristol, CT to Hartford, CT and when he removed from Hartford, CT for Brooklyn, NY.  According to Stephen Terry's family history, John Burnham fathered six
2 CONC  children through his first wife, Amanda Joanna, all but the last of which were born in Bristol, CT.  Thus, John Burnham was probably in Bristol, CT at least through 1837, the year Mary Jane Terry was born.  (His last child, Henri
2 CONC etta Amanda Terry, is listed as being born in Hartford, CT on March 5, 1846.) 
2 CONT A better indication of when John Burnham moved to, and  left, Hartford, CT can be gleaned from the various Hartford (CT) city directories, published continuously since early 1852.  A Dr. J. B. Terry" first appears in the 1855-1856
2 CONC  edition of "Geer's Hartford City Directory".  That year, he is listed as being housed at 2 Winthrop Steet.  The following year, Dr. J. B. Terry is listed as being housed at 18 Pleasant Street, where he remained through the rest o
2 CONC f his run in the Hartford (CT) city directories.  Dr. J. B. Terry last appeared in the 1862 edition of the directory.  Starting with the 1863-1864 edition, he is no longer listed as being a Hartford, CT resident.  Thus, one might 
2 CONC surmise that John Burnham Terry moved to Hartford, CT sometime in the period 1854-1855, and that he left Hartford, CT in the period 1862-1863.  Although it is likely that John Burnham immediately moved to Brooklyn, NY after leavin
2 CONC g Hartford, CT such a move is unconfirmed at this time.  Although the Connecticut State Library contains the New York City directories for the 1860's, Brooklyn, NY was a separate city in that period, as it was not incorporated int
2 CONC o New York City until the Greater New York Act of 1898.  Until Brooklyn (NY) city directories can be found for the 1860's, we can only speculate on which year John Burnham moved to Brooklyn, NY.  
2 CONT The various editions of "Geer's Hartford City Directory" that are housed in the State Library has added the title "Dr.", no one with that name is included in the list of dentists contained in the various directories.  Nor is anyon
2 CONC e named Terry included in the list of doctors or physicians.  Why J. B. Terry is not listed among the dentist is unknown.  Perhaps he practiced outside of the City of Hartford (Ct).   Perhaps there was a charge to be listed by pro
2 CONC fession and he did not feel the value of advertisement was worth the cost for the listing.  Or, perhaps, for some reason, John Burnham Terry simply stopped practicing dentistry during that period.  
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT There are no known chidren from John Burnham  Terry's second marriage (to Hannah (Clapp) Burnham/Terry) "    ,
2 CONT
2 CONT John Burnham and Amanda Johanna (Warner) Terry were married June 1, 1828 by Rev. Noah Porter. Presumably in Farmington, Ct because of the listing in the Farmington Vital Records 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Pleasant Street Property, however, did not mark John Burnham's first appearance in the Hartford (CT) land records.  Four times between 1846 and 1853 he purchased various furnishings and office equipment on credit, the transact
2 CONC ion being listed as "chattel" on the city's land records.  Over the years, he also had several different mortgages on the Pleasant Street porperty.  In addition, in 1857, John Burnham and his wife, Hannah, waived their claim to an
2 CONC y interest in a parcel of land on Ann Street sold to Orrin D. Woodruff by Isaac Hills.  In the transaction, Hills was a acting as the trustee of Leonard S. and Edward M. Burnham, who were apparently minor sons of Hannah's first Hu
2 CONC sband.,
2 CONT
2 CONT John Burnham Terry was a Dentist and Homeopathic Physician in Hartford, CT and then later in Brooklyn, NY.,
2 CONT
2 CONT "Historical and Human Interest Notes on John Burnham Terry"  by William L. Willard, 3/3/1997
2 CONT
2 CONT "John Burnham Terry (Samuel 5, Samuel 4, Samuel 3, Ephraim 2, Samuel 1, Samuel 0) was born in East Windsor, (now South Windsor) Connecticut on May 7, 1806, the son of Samuel Terry and Esther (Gillett Terry at the family residence 
2 CONC on what is now Main Street, across the street from the "ancient" burial ground.  It is most probable that during his first twelve years which were spent in East Windsor, John Burnham helped his father and grandfather manage the fa
2 CONC mily farm and harness maker's business, a trade that they were both involved in.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1818, when he was only 12 years old, John Burnham's father moved and his entire family to Plymouth, Connecticut where his father went to work for Samuel's uncle, Eli Terry, the famous clock maker.  Prior to moving to Plymouth, 
2 CONC Samuel had built a tower clock for the First Congregational Church in East Windsor, and John Burnham, along with his other brothers surely helped their father in this project, including the clock's installation in the front edific
2 CONC e of the church building. This would have given the boys early exposure to the clock business.
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon arriving in Plymouth, Samuel went to work with his brother, Eli, in the latter's clock business and around 1825, Samuel, Sr. purchased land with buildings in Bristol, Connecticut where he intended to open his own clock shop. 
2 CONC  John Burnham along with his brother Ralph were sent ahead to manage the family's newly formed concern.
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that the brothers continued to work with their father until he retired when they formed a partnership "R. & J.B. Terry" in a firm that produced brass movement 8 day shelf clocks.  This firm lasted only until 1836 when i
2 CONC t was dissolved.  Ralph Terry continued in the clock business and John Burnham pursued other interests.
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 16, 1828, John Burnham Terry married Amanda Joanna Warner, a plymouth girl.  They had six children, three of whom died young, and prior to 1846, the family moved to Hartford, Connecticut where their last child, Henrietta A
2 CONC manda was born on March 5, 1846.
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that between the years 1836 and 1846 John Burnham received some type of training in dentistry or surgery.  He is listed in the "New England Mercantile Union Business  Directory," published in 1849, as a dentist, practic
2 CONC ingat 180 1/2 Main Street, in Hartford, Connecticut.  In Elihu Geer's City Directory for the same year, he is also listed as dentist with the addition of the title "surgeon."  His office is the same being at 180 1/2 Main Street an
2 CONC d his residence is given as 4 Asylum Street, in Hartford.  In the  1857 "Connecticut Gazetteer and Business Book," he is listed as a homeopathic physician in Hartford County.  In the 1859 "Connecticut Register, he is listed again 
2 CONC as a homeopathic physician.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Horace Wells, who is credited with the first use of gas on a human being as anesthesia, was a dentist practicing in Hartford, and his office in 1845 was at 180 1/2 Main Street.  This location Dr. Wells shared with  Dr. John M.
2 CONC  Riggs, another dentist who came to Hartford in 1843.  Dr. Wells listed himself as a "surgeon dentist."  Dr. Wells died in New York City in 1848.  In the "Wells Family Genealogy," it is stated that John Burnham Terry studied denti
2 CONC stry under Dr. Wells.  This is possible as we know that John Burnham Terry was in Hartford prior to 1846, but since Dr. Wells left Hartford to go to Europe in1846, it is more likely J.B Terry started his studies under Dr. Wells bu
2 CONC t completed them with someone else.  Dr. Terry obviously continued to use the 180 1/2 Main Street address well after the death of Dr. Wells.
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 1, 1856, John Burnham's wife Amanda died and on October 15, 1856, John Burnham married as his second wife Hannah (Clapp) Burnham, widow of Leander Burnham.  Based on the "Hartford City Directories" and the "Connecticut 
2 CONC Registers," John continued to practice as a physician until 1869 when he removed to Brooklyn, New York.  Here again he is listed in the city directories as a physician, an occupation he seems to have followed until his death on Ma
2 CONC rch 14, 1870.
2 CONT
2 CONT No record of a formal education on a college level can be found for John Burnham Terry or information as to where he learned the trade as a homeopathic physician.  he probably, simply served as an apprentice until his teacher and 
2 CONC himself felt he was ready to be on his own."
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a footstone in the Isham-Terry plot (Section 12, Lot 81) of Cedar Hill Cemetery, Hartford, CT that reads "Dr. Daniel Porter, in tribute, dedicated pioneer and ancestor".  He may not be buried there since the stone is "in 
2 CONC tribute", however, he must be related to the Terry-Ishams.  The connection has not been found as of 6/16/1997.  See  photos for picture of memorial.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Clockmaking and Other Enterprises of Samuel Terry's Descendants
2 CONT John Burnham Terry (1806-1870), the fourth son, was a partner in the brass clock manufacturing firm of R. & J.B. Terry before moving to Hartford where he prospered in the dental profession."
2 CONT
2 CONT CHURCH MEMBERSHIP: Admitted to the First Congregational Church, Bristol, CT on 3/19/1843 by Profession,,
2 CONT
2 CONT Following are excerpts from reminiscences written in 1934 by Isabelle Webster (Terry) Fletcher (1878-1971) daughter of Franklin Ensign Terry.
2 CONT Franklin Ensign Terry was born 1845 in this house (the Samuel Terry homestead).  In two years his parents moved ot the little house on Preston Street, Forestville (where the Prestons lived when I was a girl.  Auntie Anna was born 
2 CONC there and when she was three years old they moved back to this house again, where Papa lived until he died in 1926 on his birthday July 7.  They always said he was a tall and slim lad, at times almost delicate looking, but I never
2 CONC  heard that he had any serious illness...
2 CONT My great grandfather (Samuel Terry) bought this house in 1828, and they made some alterations just what I do not know as the Botsfords changed it some too.  I think he tore out the big chimney and did away with the old fireplaces 
2 CONC which were in each front room and in the old long dining room.  The chimney went up in the front hall.  After this was taken out the chimney only came up in to the garret and stove pipes had to go through the bedrooms to the attic
2 CONC  chimney.  This had some advantages as they warmed the rooms they passed through.
2 CONT Samuel Terry was a brother of Eli Terry the clock maker.  He worked with his brother at one time and later made clocks in his own factory also.  At one time he made clocks down here near the river in what we called the old red sho
2 CONC p (although I never saw the original building which burned down before I was born).  He made the first clock in the Congregational Church tower.  Grandpa Terry (Ralph) [was] also down in what was once the old Andrews shop near the
2 CONC  river just off Preston St., Forestville.  At one time Samuel Terry's children lived around here in various houses.  Samuel Terry Jr. lived in the Beach Lumber Company's house - Edward in Daly's - John in the Hume place - Theodore
2 CONC  in the old Mitchel place with the pillars and one over on Pine St. where the Garey's lived...
2 CONT Papa worked in the old Welch Clock shop which stood near the railroad tracks near the Forestville station.  He turned pinions for a long while and thoroughly understood a clock movement.  Mr. Henry Bancroft and also his father...
2 CONT At the time I was married June 2, 1897 Papa was working in Winsted, [sic] Connecticut in the clock shop.  His father had worked there years before and had been foreman of the movements department.  Emerson (Terry) also worked ther
2 CONC e at the same time as his father at the age of sixteen.  Papa had been out of work here in Forestville for a long time.  There was a great depression...
2 CONT Grandpa Terry could always fix most anything that got out of order and also make new things and he always had the tools to do it.  If he did not have them he could make them.  There have been many tools here in the place that were
2 CONC  homemade.  Papa was the same way, he could do almost anything and was very particular...
2 CONT Papa and Mamma were married Aug. 2, 1870 eight years later Jan. 6, 1878 I came to town...
2 CONT In 1893 March Fred [Fletcher, Isabella's future husband] came East stopping at the World's Fair Grounds.  The Fair had not  yet opened.  He came to work for his Uncle Samuel Monce who owned  a business conducted in the old shop wh
2 CONC ich stood near the river where the Bell shop formerly stood.  The Bell shop was dismantled last year (1933).  He was  twenty years old...It was not until 1895 or so that I began to keep company with Fred...After graduating I becam
2 CONC e formally engaged and after a year I was married, June 2, 1897, here at home in the South Parlor, the same room in which my Great Grandmother Hubbell had been married...
2 CONT The factory [old shop near the river noted above] had burned down in Bristol and his uncle had moved the business to his own factory in Unionville.  We lived in Unionville three years.  Arthur was born there on April 15, 1898 in t
2 CONC he house on Farmington Ave. owned by Mr. Freeborn Alger, I have pleasant recollections of my home there and I made many friends...
2 CONT In 1900 Mamma was hurt by our horse and that year I came back to Bristol to take care of her eventually we moved here for Fred was dissatisfied in the way his uncle treated him in the business.  He was only getting two dollars a d
2 CONC ay when we were married and he had only one raise after that of twenty-five cents.
2 CONT Fred had been inventing a new glass cutter with interchangeable wheels.  This he offered to his Uncle but got no encouragement in regard to his adopting it.  Fred wanted to go into business for himself and after coming back home t
2 CONC o live Papa got Fred a position in the clock shop in Forestville.  In the fall of 1901 and into 1901 Fred started a business in the upper barn here on the place.  He worked nights here and held his place at the clock shop.  Then h
2 CONC e had to get someone to work here all the time.  These were bad years for him and he had to work hard chores and all made him busy.
2 CONT Papa was working at the Clock Shop too and it was about this time or a little later that Mr. Sessions gave Papa a good position.  Superintendent of the Movement department.  At last Papa was working so many years at the bench (all
2 CONC  at clock work) had a good position and one he was perfectly capable of filling efficiently.  He thoroughly understood clock movements both how to make them and making them run.  He could always fix a clock.
2 CONT He held his position until several years later when he resigned and gave all his time to our own business which had been organized and run under the name "The Fletcher-Terry Co."  It was a long hard pull in those years and Fred ha
2 CONC d to work very hard.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Samuel Terry built three shops on the south side of the Pequabuck River, in Bristol, near the site of the present Rolling Mill dam, and carried on quite extemsively for those days.  He retired in 1834, but he evidently did not lo
2 CONC se sight of the future of Bristol, for, when the railroad was planned, he sold the company a piece of land.  This is shown in the Bristol Land Records.  
2 CONT After Samuel, fifth, retired, the business was carried on by two of his sons, under name of Ralph E. and John B. Terry through 1935 and 1936.  At that time, John withdrew to take up the study of medicine.
2 CONT The business went on under the name of Terry & Andrews from 1842 to 1850."
