2 CONT OCCUPATION:
2 CONT "Theodore Terry was a clock manufacturer.  He began his own clockmaking enterprise, Terry & Andrews with his brother Ralph and Franklin C. Andrews in 1842.  This firm was moved to Ansonia, CT in 1850 and reorganized as the Ansonia
2 CONC  Clock Co. until the firm was destroyed by fire in the fall of 1854.  In 1854 he formed a partnership with P.T. Barnum, the great showman, known as the Terry & Barnham Manufacturing Co.  They were able to produce few clocks and we
2 CONC nt bankrupt in 1856.  Terry purchased Barnum's interest and he went to Terryville, CT to take over the operation of the Terryville Manufacturing Company which he had been the president of for several years.  About 1855 he changed 
2 CONC the firm's name to Theodore Terry & Co. and they made balance escapement clocks until about 1859.  He also was postmaster of Pequabuck, CT during this period.  Soon after 1860 he moved to Titusville, PA and was involved in the oil
2 CONC  business for a time.  He then moved back to Connecticut and probably worked for the New Haven Clock Company."    
2 CONT
2 CONT RESIDENCE:  Theodore Terry was listed in (Source:  Theodore Terry to Daniel Blackburn, Vol. 28, Page 227, January 3, 1860, Land Records, Bristol, Connecticut Town Clerk’s Office. (Pine Street, Bristol, Connecticut 06010)(Lot #1A/2
2 CONC )(Accurate Forging-Emerson Gillett Terry Home)) as being of Plymouth, CT on January 3, 1860. 
2 CONT He was listed in (Source:  Theodore Terry to Johanna Blackburn, Vol. 29, Page 553, September 27, 1865, Land Records, Bristol, Connecticut Town Clerk’s Office. (Pine Street, Bristol, Connecticut 06010)(Lot #1A/2)(Accurate Forging-E
2 CONC merson Gillett Terry Home) as "Theodore Terry of Holyoke, Massachusetts being now in Bristol."  
2 CONT Terryville, CT to Titusville, PA, to New Haven, CT. 
2 CONT
