1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT - and list his date of birth as 3/4/1812. According to,, and  Edward Terry died on 8/22/1866.
2 CONT
2 CONT RESIDENCE:  Went to Virginia for a time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT CHURCH MEMBERSHIP:  According  to & Edward became a member of the First Congregational Church, Bristol, Ct ---Admission By Profession on 3/19/1843.  According to  page 81 Edward and his wife, Ann (Lewis) Terry had a "Letter from W
2 CONC instead, CT",. According to Edward and his wife Ann were admitted 8/12/1855 with a "Letter from Winsted, CT".
2 CONT
2 CONT DATE OF DEATH AND AGE AT DEATH:  54 years old:  List the date 1866
2 CONT "In Southington, Aug. 22d, Edward Terry, aged 54 years."
2 CONT
2 CONT OTHER PROPERTIES OWNED:
2 CONT On February 2, 1832, Grantor Henry Tompkins of Bristol executed a warrantee deed to Grantees Samuel S. Terry, Ralph Terry, John B. Terry, Theodore Terry, and Edward Terry all of Bristol for sixty three dollars for land described a
2 CONC s follows:
2 CONT “[A] certain piece of land situate in said Bristol, containing thirty five rods more or less, bounding northeasterly by the River as it passes the house of Philo Pierce, southerly on land of Ashabel Mix and westerly on highway.”
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:  Henry Tompkins to Samuel S. Terry, Ralph Terry, John B. Terry, Theodore Terry, Edward Terry, Vol. 16, Page 311, February 2, 1832, Land Records, Bristol, Connecticut Town Clerk’s Office. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Edward lived in what was later the William Daly House  He moved to Virginia later.  In after years his son, George, came north for his education and became a well-known lawyer and business executive of Waterbury."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Most fascinating of the gaps in the Terry history are those that surround Edward Terry.  He was born in South Windsor, CT on March 4, 1812 and died on August 22, 1866.  Edward had married someone Stephen Terry listed as Anne Lewi
2 CONC s on March 25, 1833.  Stephen Terry lists Anne Lewis as being born in Wolcott, CT on January 25, 1812 and dying in Waterbury, CT on September 6, 1868.  It was not known where Edward and his wife were buried.  Nor was any informati
2 CONC on on Edward's in-laws available.  
2 CONT A search of Connecticut burial records quickly uncovered the graves of Edward and his wife.  They are both buried in Riverside (now West) Cemetery on Route 177 in Plainville, CT.  An examination of the headstones reveals that Edwa
2 CONC rd's wife probably spelled her name "Ann", rather than "Anne".  (Other documents, including the listing of Edward's marriage in the Southington vital records, as well as property in Southington's land records, confirms that Edward
2 CONC 's wife spelled her name without the "E".) 
2 CONT An inspection of the grave site revealed that a total of six people, four of those whose connection to Edward Terry is unknown at this time, are buried in the plot.  Marking the graves are four, identical stones, set next to each 
2 CONC other, with the stone closest to the cemetery road marking the grave of Ann (Lewis) Terry.  The inscriptions of the four stones read as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT ANN LEWIS
2 CONT WIDOW OF
2 CONT EDWARD TERRY
2 CONT BORN JAN. 25, 1812
2 CONT DIED SEPT. 6, 1868
2 CONT
2 CONT EDWARD TERRY
2 CONT DIED
2 CONT AUG 22, 1866
2 CONT AGED 54
2 CONT
2 CONT DANIEL B. EVERITT
2 CONT DIED IN WARREN, CT
2 CONT JUNE 10, 1841
2 CONT AGED 45 YRS
2 CONT PHEBE STRONG
2 CONT WIFE OF
2 CONT DANIEL B. EVERITT
2 CONT DIED FEB. 21 1878
2 CONT AGED 79 YRS
2 CONT
2 CONT Forever With the Lord
2 CONT LYDIA EVERITT
2 CONT WIFE OF LEVI E. MOULTHROP
2 CONT DIED DEC. 6, 1862
2 CONT AGED 29 YRS.
2 CONT Come unto me all ye that
2 CONT labour and are heavy laden
2 CONT and I will give you rest
2 CONT CARLIE
2 CONT THEIR ONLY SON
2 CONT DIED DEC. 10, 1862
2 CONT AGED 9 MOS
2 CONT
2 CONT That the first two stones are that of Samuel's son Edward and Edward's wife are without a doubt.  The dates listed on the markers agree with the information in Stephen Terry's genealogy.  What relation the other four people have t
2 CONC o the Terry family has not yet been determined.  Although Daniel B. Everitt is the first of these people to die, he may not have been the first to be buried in the plot.  His stone reveals that he actually died in Warren, CT, appr
2 CONC oximately fourty miles west of Plainville.  Connecticut cemetery records reveals that a stone in a cemetery in Warren, CT also marks his burial site.  Thus, it seems that Daniel Everitt was removed from his burial place in Warren 
2 CONC and re-interred in Plainville.  Lydia (Everitt) Moulthroup could be Daniel's sister, but she is more likely his daughter.  Judging by the placement of the tombs (with those dying first plaved farthest from the road), it would seem
2 CONC  that Daniel Everitt was reburied in Plainville following the death and burial of Lydia, rather than the death and burial of his wife, Phebe.  
2 CONT The connection between the Everitts and Edward Terry has not yet been determined, though the style and placement of the grave stones would indicate that some type of connection does indeed exist.  Unrelated families were sometimes
2 CONC  connected by the marriage of their children.  However, as far as we know, Edward and Ann only had one child, a son named George E. Terry who was born in Bristol, CT on September 15, 1836.  The Everitt's were not George E. Terry's
2 CONC  in-laws as he originally married a woman named Emma Pollard and, after her death, he married a woman named Fannie E. Williams.  The Everitts are not immediate relatives of Edward's wife, either, as recent research has, for the fi
2 CONC rst time, uncovered the parents of Ann Lewis.  Perhaps the Everitts were connected to the Terrys through business or close personal friendship. 
2 CONT The discovery of Edward and Ann's grave site in Plainville raised additional questions.  Edward is thought to have been an attorney in Waterbury.  Why would he and his wife have been buried in Plainville?  News of Edward's death a
2 CONC ppeared in the "Waterbury Daily American" on Tuesday, August 28, 1866.  Unfortunately, report was more of a death notice than an obituary.  The entire notice, in its entirety, read as follows: 
2 CONT
2 CONT DIED
2 CONT In Southington, Aug. 22d, Edward Terry, aged 54 years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the notice disappoints in not providing us with any information on Edward's family or business, it does provide us with one key piece of information -- Edward died in Southington, the town where he and Ann were married.  
2 CONC The fact that Edward seemed to maintain ties to Southington could explain his burial in Plainville, as the town of Southington has along been economically and geographically tied to nearby Plainville.  
2 CONT As was stated above, one of the missing details about Edward Terry's life was the name of his in-laws.  We knew that he married a woman named Ann Lewis from Wolcott, CT in 1833.  We also knew that the marriage took place in Southi
2 CONC ngton, CT.  Other than that, however, the facts surrounding Ann Lewis had always been a mystery.  Statewide birth records do not have a record for the birth of an Ann Lewis in 1812.  However, a check of Southington land records ha
2 CONC s finally uncovered information that leads to  Ann's family tree.  
2 CONT An 1834 quit claim deed from someone named Newel Lewis makes contains the following information:
2 CONT Know all men by these presents, that I, Newel Lewis, formerly of Wolcott in the County of New Haven and State of Connecticut, now residing in the State of Alabama, being one of the grandchildren and heirs at law of Pomeroy Newell 
2 CONC late of Southington in the County of Hartford and State of Connecticut deceased -- for divers good cause and consideration thereunto {unintelligible} especially for the love and consideration I have towards my sisters, Ann Terry o
2 CONC f Bristol in the County of Hartford, and Electa Terry of Wolcott aforesaid....(SLR, Vol. 15, Page 531)
2 CONT Thus, from this deed, in which Newel Lewis leaves the property he inherited from his grandfather, to his sisters, Ann Terry and Electa Lewis, we can determine the names of a brother and sister to Ann Lewis.  Unfortuanately, there 
2 CONC is no record in the state birth records of the birth of a Newel Lewis or Electa Lewis  who are of the proper age to be a sibling of Ann Lewis.  The deed gives us another name which did prove useful.  That of Ann's grandfather, Pom
2 CONC eroy Newell.  
2 CONT Pomeroy Newell is obviously Ann (Lewis) Terry's maternal grandfather, as her paternal grandfather would have to have bore the surname Lewis.  Pomeroy Newell must have been a very prominent man in early 19th century Connecticut.  N
2 CONC ews of his death, which came at age 86 on October 21, 1831, a time when few obituaries were printed, was carried by no less than eight newspapers, including the following "The Hartford Courant" (Nov. 1, 1831), "The Litchfield Post
2 CONC  & Enquirer" (Nov. 3, 1831), "The National Republican" (Nov. 5, 1831), "The Connecticut Journal & New Haven Post-Boy" (Nov. 8, 1831), "The Connecticut Herald" (Nov. 8, 1831), and the "New Haven Religious Intelliger" (Nov. 19, 1831
2 CONC ).  A search of Southington church records reveals that four children of Pomeroy Newell were baptized on January 15, 1786.  The children were Alecta, Roxana, Lucina, & Elisabeth Newell.  (Southington First Church Records, Vol. 2, 
2 CONC page 61).  The odds are very strong that one of these girls is the mother of Ann Lewis.  A further check of Southington chruch records showed that Alecta Newell married a man named Rice Lewis on Dec. 21, 1796.  (Southington First 
2 CONC Chruch Records, Vol. 2, page 128.)  Although the lack of birth records fails to confirm it, it is highly likely that Rice Lewis and Alecta Newell Lewis were the parents of Ann Lewis, as well as the parents of Newel Lewis and Elect
2 CONC a Lewis.   
2 CONT Research was also undertaken on the only known child of Edward and Ann Terry, George E. Terry.  As was stated above, George E. Terry was married twice.  His first wife, Emma Pollard, was born in Loughborough, England and died in W
2 CONC aterbury, CT on April 27, 1868.  Eleven months after his first wife's death, George E. married Fannie E. WIlliams, who was born in Bristol, CT on December 1, 1843.  Cursory research into Fannie E. Williams has yet to uncover the n
2 CONC ames of her parents, though there were a fairly large number of Williamses living in Bristol in the first half of the 19th century.  As for Emma Pollard, according to the 1860 United States Census, two women of that name lived in 
2 CONC Connecticut in that year, one in Colchester and the other in New Haven.  Neither proved to be the woman who became George E. Terry's wife.  Many, but by no means all, of the passenger manifests for 19th century trans-Atlantic trav
2 CONC el have been catalogued.  A check on the 12 volume index to these passenger manifests has also failed to turn up a passenger named Emma Pollard.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Edward and Ann (Lewis) Terry are buried in West Cemetery, Plainville.  Their two tombstones reside next to two other identical tombstones; one for Daniel B. Everitt and his wife Phebe Strong, and one for Lydia (Everitt) Moulthrop
2 CONC  and her son Carlie.  The inscriptions on the four stones reads as follows:
2 CONT ANN LEWIS
2 CONT WIDOW OF
2 CONT EDWARD TERRY
2 CONT BORN JAN. 25, 1812
2 CONT DIED SEPT. 6, 1868
2 CONT
2 CONT EDWARD TERRY
2 CONT DIED
2 CONT AUG. 22, 1866
2 CONT AGED 54
2 CONT
2 CONT DANIEL B. EVERITT
2 CONT DIED IN WARREN, CT
2 CONT JUNE 10, 1841
2 CONT AGED 45 YEARS
2 CONT PHEBE STRONG
2 CONT WIFE OF
2 CONT DANIEL B. EVERITT
2 CONT DIED FEB. 21, 1878
2 CONT AGED 79 YEARS
2 CONT
2 CONT Forever With The Lord
2 CONT LYDIA EVERITT
2 CONT WIFE OF LEVI E. MOULTHROP
2 CONT DIED
2 CONT DEC. 6, 1862
2 CONT AGED 29 YEARS
2 CONT Come unto me all ye that
2 CONT labour and are heavy laden
2 CONT and i will give you rest
2 CONT CARLIE
2 CONT THEIR ONLY SON
2 CONT DIED DEC. 10, 1862
2 CONT AGED 9 MOS.  (also from)
2 CONT
2 CONT Given the identical nature of the four stones, it would appear that some sort of relationship existed between the Terrys and the Everitt-Moulthrops.  The nature of that relationship is still a mystery.  The history of the Terry fa
2 CONC mily has been well-documented in other reports.  Below is a brief outline of the known history of the Everitt-Moulthrop family.  
2 CONT Daniel B. Everitt
2 CONT As stated on his stone, Daniel B. Everitt died in 1841 at age 45.  Little is known of his life, including his birth or parentage.  His tombstone in Plainville states that he died on June 10, 1841 in Warren, CT.  According to Conne
2 CONC cticut cemetery records, there is also a tombstone that bears his name in the town of Warren, CT.  (Warren, 622, CT, Cem. 1, Page 16).  Apparently, he was removed from his resting place in Warren to be buried with his wife and dau
2 CONC ghter in Plainville.  
2 CONT Phebe (Strong) Everitt
2 CONT The widow of Daniel B. Everitt, Phebe (Strong) Everitt apparently died in the Plainville area in 1878.  Her move from Warren to Plainville can be pinpointed through church records.  Records show that the Widow Everitt was admitted
2 CONC  to the Congregational Church in Warren on March 5, 1855 (Warren Church of Christ Records, Vol. 5, page 621), and that she was dismissed from that congregation and recommended for membership to the church in Plainville on March 29
2 CONC , 1861.   (Warren Church of Christ Records, Vol. 5, Page 272.)  Along with Mrs. Phebe Everitt, the Warren Church recommended the admittance of Miss Laura Everitt and Miss Huldah Everitt for admission to Plainville.  Thus, it seems
2 CONC  that Phebe Everitt was accompanied to Plainville with her daughters or, possibly, step-daughters.  
2 CONT Lydia (Everitt) Moulthrop
2 CONT Lydia Everitt, the daughter of Daniel B. Everitt (and, likely, the daughter of Phebe Strong Everitt, as well) married Levi Moulthrop of Farmington on September 18, 1860.  ("Litchfield County Press & Enquirer", September 20, 1860, 
2 CONC page 384.)  The wedding took place at the Warren Congregational Church (Warren Church of Christ Records, Vol. 5, page 240).
2 CONT The exact identity of Levi Moulthrop is still unknown, but the family name appears to have been quite prominent in the Bristol area.  A Civil War veteran named Newell Moulthrop (born on Feb. 29, 1840, died, January 26, 1929) is bu
2 CONC ried in West Cemetery in Bristol.  Newell Moulthrop was born in Wolcott to Sherman Moulthrop.  As Ann (Lewis) Terry was also born in Wolcott, perhaps the connection between the Terrys and the Everitts lies with Ann and Levi.  Thou
2 CONC gh it may very well be a coincidence, it is also interesting to note that Newell Moulthrop could have been named after Ann's grandfather, Pomeroy Newell.  Newell Moulthrop's son, George E. Moulthrop, was a noted photographer who c
2 CONC aptured many scenes from Bristol's past.  
2 CONT Lydia died on December 6, 1862.  Her nine month old son Carlie died four days later.  They appear to be the first people buried in the plot.  The final resting place of her husband, Levi Moulthrop, is unknown at this time." 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Clockmaking and Other Enterprises of Samuel Terry's Descendants
2 CONT Samuel Terry's sons William (1800-1822), Edward (1812-1866) and Milo Terry (1814-1870) were lesser known clockmakers, their working careers spent in the employ of other clockmaking firms."
2 CONT
2 CONT Following are excerpts from reminiscences written in 1934 by Isabelle Webster (Terry) Fletcher (1878-1971) daughter of Franklin Ensign Terry.
2 CONT Franklin Ensign Terry was born 1845 in this house (the Samuel Terry homestead).  In two years his parents moved ot the little house on Preston Street, Forestville (where the Prestons lived when I was a girl.  Auntie Anna was born 
2 CONC there and when she was three years old they moved back to this house again, where Papa lived until he died in 1926 on his birthday July 7.  They always said he was a tall and slim lad, at times almost delicate looking, but I never
2 CONC  heard that he had any serious illness...
2 CONT My great grandfather (Samuel Terry) bought this house in 1828, and they made some alterations just what I do not know as the Botsfords changed it some too.  I think he tore out the big chimney and did away with the old fireplaces 
2 CONC which were in each front room and in the old long dining room.  The chimney went up in the front hall.  After this was taken out the chimney only came up in to the garret and stove pipes had to go through the bedrooms to the attic
2 CONC  chimney.  This had some advantages as they warmed the rooms they passed through.
2 CONT Samuel Terry was a brother of Eli Terry the clock maker.  He worked with his brother at one time and later made clocks in his own factory also.  At one time he made clocks down here near the river in what we called the old red sho
2 CONC p (although I never saw the original building which burned down before I was born).  He made the first clock in the Congregational Church tower.  Grandpa Terry (Ralph) [was] also down in what was once the old Andrews shop near the
2 CONC  river just off Preston St., Forestville.  At one time Samuel Terry's children lived around here in various houses.  Samuel Terry Jr. lived in the Beach Lumber Company's house - Edward in Daly's - John in the Hume place - Theodore
2 CONC  in the old Mitchel place with the pillars and one over on Pine St. where the Garey's lived...
2 CONT Papa worked in the old Welch Clock shop which stood near the railroad tracks near the Forestville station.  He turned pinions for a long while and thoroughly understood a clock movement.  Mr. Henry Bancroft and also his father...
2 CONT At the time I was married June 2, 1897 Papa was working in Winsted, [sic] Connecticut in the clock shop.  His father had worked there years before and had been foreman of the movements department.  Emerson (Terry) also worked ther
2 CONC e at the same time as his father at the age of sixteen.  Papa had been out of work here in Forestville for a long time.  There was a great depression...
2 CONT Grandpa Terry could always fix most anything that got out of order and also make new things and he always had the tools to do it.  If he did not have them he could make them.  There have been many tools here in the place that were
2 CONC  homemade.  Papa was the same way, he could do almost anything and was very particular...
2 CONT Papa and Mamma were married Aug. 2, 1870 eight years later Jan. 6, 1878 I came to town...
2 CONT In 1893 March Fred [Fletcher, Isabella's future husband] came East stopping at the World's Fair Grounds.  The Fair had not  yet opened.  He came to work for his Uncle Samuel Monce who owned  a business conducted in the old shop wh
2 CONC ich stood near the river where the Bell shop formerly stood.  The Bell shop was dismantled last year (1933).  He was  twenty years old...It was not until 1895 or so that I began to keep company with Fred...After graduating I becam
2 CONC e formally engaged and after a year I was married, June 2, 1897, here at home in the South Parlor, the same room in which my Great Grandmother Hubbell had been married...
2 CONT The factory [old shop near the river noted above] had burned down in Bristol and his uncle had moved the business to his own factory in Unionville.  We lived in Unionville three years.  Arthur was born there on April 15, 1898 in t
2 CONC he house on Farmington Ave. owned by Mr. Freeborn Alger, I have pleasant recollections of my home there and I made many friends...
2 CONT In 1900 Mamma was hurt by our horse and that year I came back to Bristol to take care of her eventually we moved here for Fred was dissatisfied in the way his uncle treated him in the business.  He was only getting two dollars a d
2 CONC ay when we were married and he had only one raise after that of twenty-five cents.
2 CONT Fred had been inventing a new glass cutter with interchangeable wheels.  This he offered to his Uncle but got no encouragement in regard to his adopting it.  Fred wanted to go into business for himself and after coming back home t
2 CONC o live Papa got Fred a position in the clock shop in Forestville.  In the fall of 1901 and into 1901 Fred started a business in the upper barn here on the place.  He worked nights here and held his place at the clock shop.  Then h
2 CONC e had to get someone to work here all the time.  These were bad years for him and he had to work hard chores and all made him busy.
2 CONT Papa was working at the Clock Shop too and it was about this time or a little later that Mr. Sessions gave Papa a good position.  Superintendent of the Movement department.  At last Papa was working so many years at the bench (all
2 CONC  at clock work) had a good position and one he was perfectly capable of filling efficiently.  He thoroughly understood clock movements both how to make them and making them run.  He could always fix a clock.
2 CONT He held his position until several years later when he resigned and gave all his time to our own business which had been organized and run under the name "The Fletcher-Terry Co."  It was a long hard pull in those years and Fred ha
2 CONC d to work very hard.
2 CONT Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000185360628981label=@S86@
