2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 DEC 1855
2 PLAC St. Paul, Minnesota
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT - lists his date of death as 9 Dec 1855, whereas lists his death date as 10 Dec 1855.
2 CONT
2 CONT Records of the Mormon Family History Center in Bloomfield, CT reveal that Franklin Kellogg Terry was a "relative" of someone named Charles Alphonso Terry.  The nature of the relationship was not idicated.  Perhaps further research
2 CONC  into the Mormon records can uncover lost information on Franklin Kellogg Terry, who seemed to lose contact with the Connecticut Terrys after he moved West.                                                                          
2 CONC
2 CONT
2 CONT RESIDENCE:  Removed from Albany, N.Y. to Minnesota. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "The 1850 United States Census states that Franklin Kellogg, his wife and an African-American servant lived in the village of Coeymans, New York, in Albany County, about 15 miles south of the city of Albany.  The following informa
2 CONC tion was recorded in the census for the three people in the Terry household (Source 1850 US Census, Roll 472, Albany County, NY, Village of Coeymans, Page 238.
2 CONT
2 CONT Franklin K. Terry , age 34, male, white, born in CT, Counsellor of Law, Value of property is $2000.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jane H. Terry, age 28, female, white, born in MA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harriet Pentors, age 28, female, black, born in NY.
2 CONT
2 CONT Interestingly, either the census failed ot record the proper first name of Franklin Kellogg's wife of she dropped it from her name.
2 CONT An examination of Albany, New York city directories for the 1850's reveals that Franklin Kellogg did not establish himself in that community until later in the decade.  Perhaps it took him a few years to earn inclusion in the dire
2 CONC ctory.  Or, more likely, perhaps in 1850 he was only a village lawyer in Coeymans, and he moved his practice to Albany later.  Whatever the reason, Franklin Kellogg finally turns up in the 1852-1853 city directory.  The directory 
2 CONC lists him as an "atty. &c." practicing at 82 State Street, with his residence listed as "boards, City Hotel." (Hoffman's Albany Directory & City Register, published by L. C. Hoffman, Albany, NY, 1852, page 303.)  Perhaps, in 1852,
2 CONC  Franklin Kellogg maintained his family residence in Coeymans, and rented an apartment at the City Hotel to use when conducting business in Albany.  
2 CONT State Street was, and still is, an important avenue in Albany.  It is about four blocks long, running form the Hudson River on the East, up the hill to the State Capitol on the West.  Along State Street, near the river, is the cit
2 CONC y's commerical and financial district.  Closer to the capital stood the state's government offices.  Attorney's based on State Street were well positioned to do business on either, or both, ends of the roadway.  In the next three 
2 CONC years, Franklin Kellogg 's business address changed three times, but only once did it venture from State Street.  In 1853, the "Directory to the Trades" listed his practice at 74 State Street.  ("Directory to the Trades in Albany 
2 CONC for the Year 1853", published by J. Munsell, Albany, 1853, page 2).  The following year, he practiced at 480 Broadway,.  ("Directory to the Trades in Albany for the Year 1854", published by J. Munsell, Albany, 1854, page 317.)  Fi
2 CONC nally, in 1855, his last year in Albany, Franklin Kellogg Terry returned to State Street, practicing at 77 State Street.  ("Directory to the Trades in Albany for the Year 1855", published by J. Munsell, Albany, 1855, page 336.)
2 CONT The city directories also revealed that, although Franklin Kellogg Terry originally practiced alone, he worked with two different partners before leaving the city.  The 1854 address, at 480 Broadway, was for the law offices of Ste
2 CONC vens & Terry. The following year, Franklin Kellogg was no longer a partner in that firm, but instead practiced at 77 State Street in the firm of Terry & Lawton. 
2 CONT Apparently, Franklin Kellogg Terry only boarded at the City Hotel for one year.  The 1853-54 city directory states that Franklin Kellogg had a home at 659 Broadway.  In 1854, Terry moved his family to a house at 111 State Street.
2 CONT Although the United States Federal Census is only conducted on years ending in "0" for over a century, the State of New York conducted a census of its own in years ending in "5."  It was from the 1855 New York State Census that th
2 CONC e following additional information on Franklin Kellogg Terry's household was gleaned (1855 New York State Census, Albany, NY, Western Half of Fifth Ward, Page 45.):
2 CONT F.K. Terry, 39 years, male, born in CT, lawyer, lived 4 years in New York.
2 CONT J.M.H. Terry, 34 years, female, wife, born in MA, lived 4 years in New York
2 CONT J. Terry, age 15, female, niece, born in CT
2 CONT Nora Farkin, age 25, female, servant, born in Ireland
2 CONT Bridget Keyon, age 25, female, servant, born in Ireland
2 CONT
2 CONT The census also stated that the Terry family lived in a brick house that was valued at $15,000.
2 CONT The information in the New York State Census is even more useful than that found in the United States Census five years earlier.  First of all, the census lists Franklin Kellogg's wife's name as "J.M.H."  This, along with the info
2 CONC rmation found in the 1850 United States Census listing her name as simply "Jane H.", leads one to speculate that perhaps Stephen Terry's book inadvertently transposed the first and middle name of Franklin Kellogg's wife.  Instead 
2 CONC of "Mary Jane Huntington," perhaps her name was "Jane Mary Huntington."
2 CONT Of prime importance is the fact that the New York State Census of 1855, like the United States Census of 1850, does not list any children living in Franklin Kellogg Terry's household.  As Franklin Kellogg only lived six months bey
2 CONC ond the date of the census, this all but confirms the belief that he died without issue.  
2 CONT Also of interest is J. Terry who, according to the Census, lived with Franklin Kellogg and his wife for two years.  Originally, the census-taker wrote down that she was Franklin's daughter, which would have meant that she was born
2 CONC  when Franklin was 25 and his wife was 19.  However, the word "daughter" was scratched out, and the word "niece" was inserted above it.  The identity of this child remains a mystery.  Because the young girl's last name is listed a
2 CONC s "Terry," it is assumed that she is from Franklin Kellogg's side of the family, and not his wife's.  A review of the known grandchildren of Samuel and Esther Terry reveals that only one girl, Juliette -- the daughter of Milo and 
2 CONC Abigail Terry, had a first name that began with a "J."  Although Milo and Abigail had six children, only the birth date of one child, Willard Milo Terry (born 1848), is known.  Milo Terry married Abigail Murphy in 1838, so it is p
2 CONC ossible that they had a child born in 1840.  However, Stephen Terry lists Juliette after Willard Milo, indicating that she probably was born after 1848.  If this is, in fact, the case, then Juliette is not the young girl living in
2 CONC  the Franklin Kellogg Terry household.  However, if Milo and Abigail's children are listed in no particular order, then it is highly probable that is was Juliette Terry who was living with her aunt and uncle in Albany in 1855.  
2 CONT Finally, the state census reveals that Franklin Kellogg was now doing well enough to afford two servant, rather than the single servant he had employed when he lived in Coeymans five years earlier.
2 CONT Stephen Terry vaguely tells us that Franklin Kellogg Terry "went West," where he died on December 10, 1855.  A search of Connecticut obituaries has pin-pointed his place of death.  Obituaries running in January 1856 issues of the 
2 CONC "Hartford Courant," "Hartford Times," and the "Christian Secretary" all state that Franklin Kellogg Terry died on December 9, 1855 in St. Paul, Minnesota.  Only the 1856 "Courant" was found on microfilm in the Connecticut State Li
2 CONC brary, and the obituary in that newspaper ran as follows:
2 CONT "In St. Paul, Minn., Dec. 9, Franklin K. Terry, formerly of Albany, N.Y., aged 40. ("The Hartford Courant," Jan. 9, 1856, page 2.)
2 CONT The question remains, what was Franklin Kellogg doing in Minnesota, a territory that did not even enter the Union until 1858, in December of 1855?  He apparently had a home and an established law practice in Albany.  One would be 
2 CONC tempted to assume that Franklin Terry was just passing through the region, when some terrible illness or accident befell him.  However, the obituary is very clear on the fact that, by the time of his death, he no longer lived in A
2 CONC lbany.  Therefore, unless more information on Franklin Kellogg's death turns up in Minnesota, we are left to only speculate on his demise.  
2 CONT Sadly, a check of 1860 U.S. Census records in Minnesota, New York, and Massachusetts failed to turn up an entry for a Mary Terry, a Mary Jane Terry, or a Jane Mary Terry.  There was a Jane Terry in Minnesota in 1860, but she was o
2 CONC nly six years old.  Perhaps Franklin Kellogg's widow was remarried before 1860, or perhaps, she, too, died before the 1850's were out."
