1 NAME Phebe /Merrill/
2 GIVN Phebe
2 _MARNM Merrill
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1810
2 ADDR
3 STAE Ontario
3 CTRY Canada
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Phoebe Merrill, daughter of Samuel and Phoebe (Lothrop) Merrill, had two husbands that we know of.  One was a Gould (as per Connie McCann Wilkes) and the other was Michael J. O'Brien (as per Missouri/Michigan marri
2 CONC age indices).  I'm pretty sure that I have marriage data from a trip to Shelbina, Missouri when I went to research John and Emeline Crandall's lives there (along with John's mother, Phoebe, and his sibling, Francis).  I remember b
2 CONC eing surprised to find the marriage certificate, but it's possible that Phoebe was there visiting her family.  Also found death information about Phoebe Merrill, Samuel's wife, John and Albert's mother, in South Dakota or Minnesot
2 CONC a.  Get this stuff straightened out!  THEN, to add to that, is the Marr marriage.  This whole thing could either come undone like a house of cards, or could be neatly tied together in a bright package--depending on the data that w
2 CONC e find.  Sigh. (note by Nancy Merrill Sayed)
2 CONT
2 CONT Okay, from what I see, they have Phebe Merril born 1810 in Canada, married to William Howard Marr in 1830, and having a daughter, born Chloe Elizabeth MARR b: 24 Sep 1832 in Hope, Ontario Co, NY.  Now, this is a problem, because P
2 CONC hoebe (married to Samuel) was born about 1807-1809 (depending on which census you look at) in the U.S.  She married Samuel, and had the following children in the 1851 census  (and note that the Phoebe Merrill they think is married
2 CONC  to Marr, is not born until 1845).  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1851 census: Note that I haven't listed Samuel Merrill, but I know he's in there--will check back through hard copies and find him:  The first person noted on this is his wife, Phibe A./Phoebe, born 1808 in the U.S.  This census i
2 CONC s from Cramahe, Northumberland County, Ontario, Canada:  Also note that Samuel and Phoebe's daughter, Phebe,/Phoebe, is only six years old in this census, born 1845.  '''It's not possible that either one of them would have been ma
2 CONC rried to William Marr in 1830, and had a daughter in 1832, because the daughter wasn't even born until 1845, and the mother was busy having daughters Bridget and Eliza between 1829 and 1831'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Phibe A Merrell Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1808 — United States residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page 69 line 15
2 CONT
2 CONT Bridget A Merrell Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1829 — Canada residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page 69 line 16
2 CONT
2 CONT Eliza A Merrell Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1831 — Canada residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page 69 line 17
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry D Merrell Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1837 — Canada residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page 69 line 18
2 CONT
2 CONT Arthiesea Merrell Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1839 — Canada residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page 69 line 19
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Merrell Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1842 — Canada residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page 69 line 20
2 CONT
2 CONT Phebe Merrell Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1845 — Canada residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page 69 line 21
2 CONT
2 CONT Ratina Merrell Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1848 — Canada residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page 69 line 22
2 CONT
2 CONT name:	John Lathrop age:	25 Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1826 -- Canada province:	Canada West (Ontario) residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page number:	69 line number:	23
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert Merrell Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1836 — Canada residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page 69 line 25
2 CONT
2 CONT There was another child born between lines 23 and 25 (will find later when I have the hard copy).  Albert was born earlier in 1836, but he's listed below the family, along with his brother John Lathrop (listed as son, in spite of 
2 CONC them retaining the middle name, and omitting the surname).  Both John and Albert were also listed separately on an agricultural census, which I think means they probably worked as farm labor for other families as well:
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert Merrell Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1835 — Canada residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada sub-district:	Cramahe sub-district number:	225 page number:	95 line number:	50 library and archives c
2 CONC anada film number:	C_11739
2 CONT
2 CONT John Merrill Canada Census, 1851 birth:	1830 — Canada residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland County, Canada West (Ontario), Canada page number:	39 line number:	37
2 CONT
2 CONT There are other Lothrop/Lathrop people in the same area, but they were either born later, or had different first times--they might have been related to Phoebe, Samuel's wife, but the John Lothrop Merrill, born about 1830, married 
2 CONC Emeline Crandall and eventually moved to Monroe and Shelby Co., Missouri.  Phoebe (nee Lothrop) Merrill, married to Samuel, lived with them in 1870, nearby in 1880, and died the household of a daughter and her husband in either th
2 CONC e MN or SD areas (will get more info on that later as well) in about 1896.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the things that helps distinguish this family from other branches (say that of Solomon Merrill, brother of Samuel, and Maria Anna [nee Lapp] Merrill, who were Methodists), was that Samuel's family was Baptist.  Some of them
2 CONC  (i.e. Roger, the father of both Samuel and Solomon) were found in the records of the Wicklow Baptist Church nearby.  See:
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles, Eve, Matilda and Silas Lathrup http://www.ontariogenealogy.com/Northumberland/wicklowbaptistchurch_3.html#L
2 CONT
2 CONT Cornelius, John, Roger (father of Samuel and Solomon) and Samantha Merill and Jackson Merrill, and Samuel Merrel are found here: http://www.ontariogenealogy.com/Northumberland/wicklowbaptistchurch_4.html#M
2 CONT
2 CONT We find the above family, that of Samuel and Phoebe Merrill (from 1851 census) listed below in the 1861 census, as Baptists (note:  they didn't list religion in 1851):
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Merrills Ontario Census, 1861 birth:	1801 — U C residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland, Ontario, Canada age:	60y estimated birth year:	1801 birthplace:	U C (Upper Canada) marital status:	Married religion:	Baptist page number:	1
2 CONC 5 line number:	23 film number:	2435928 lac film number:	C-1055 digital folder number:	4391946 image number:	00692
2 CONT
2 CONT Phebe Merrills Ontario Census, 1861 birth:	1809 — U S residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland, Ontario, Canada age:	52y estimated birth year:	1809 birthplace:	U S marital status:	Married religion:	Baptist page number:	15 line number:	2
2 CONC 4 film number:	2435928 lac film number:	C-1055 digital folder number:	4391946 image number:	00692
2 CONT
2 CONT John Merrills Ontario Census, 1861 birth:	1827 — U C residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland, Ontario, Canada age:	34y estimated birth year:	1827 birthplace:	U C marital status:	Single religion:	Baptist page number:	15 line number:	25 
2 CONC film number:	2435928 lac film number:	C-1055 digital folder number:	4391946 image number:	00692
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Merrills Ontario Census, 1861 birth:	1838 — U C residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland, Ontario, Canada age:	23y estimated birth year:	1838 birthplace:	U C marital status:	Single religion:	Baptist page number:	15 line number:	26
2 CONC  film number:	2435928 lac film number:	C-1055 digital folder number:	4391946 image number:	00692
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert Merrills Ontario Census, 1861 birth:	1843 — U C residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland, Ontario, Canada age:	18y estimated birth year:	1843 birthplace:	U C marital status:	Single religion:	Baptist page number:	15 line number:	2
2 CONC 7 film number:	2435928 lac film number:	C-1055 digital folder number:	4391946 image number:	00692 (Note:  Now why does he say he’s eighteen?  In 1859, he was  supposedly married in Fremont!  And born 1834 or 1836?)
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Merrills Ontario Census, 1861 birth:	1836 — U C residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland, Ontario, Canada age:	25y estimated birth year:	1836 birthplace:	U C marital status:	Single religion:	Baptist page number:	15 line number:	
2 CONC 28 film number:	2435928 lac film number:	C-1055 digital folder number:	4391946 image number:	00692
2 CONT
2 CONT Artemisia Merrills Ontario Census, 1861 birth:	1840 — U C residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland, Ontario, Canada age:	21y estimated birth year:	1840 birthplace:	U C marital status:	Single religion:	Baptist page number:	15 line number
2 CONC :	29 film number:	2435928 lac film number:	C-1055 digital folder number:	4391946 image number:	00692
2 CONT
2 CONT Phoebe Merrills Ontario Census, 1861 birth:	1845 — U C residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland, Ontario, Canada age:	16y estimated birth year:	1845 birthplace:	U C marital status:	Single religion:	Baptist page number:	15 line number:	3
2 CONC 0 film number:	2435928 lac film number:	C-1055 digital folder number:	4391946 image number:	00692
2 CONT
2 CONT Ratene Merrills Ontario Census, 1861 birth:	1848 — U C residence:	Cramahe, Northumberland, Ontario, Canada age:	13y estimated birth year:	1848 birthplace:	U C marital status:	Single religion:	Baptist page number:	15 line number:	3
2 CONC 1 film number:	2435928 lac film number:	C-1055 digital folder number:	4391946 image number:	00692
2 CONT
2 CONT There was also another child, about the same age as Albert, whose middle name was Forest.  He also lived in Fremont (Alpena).  Got married there, had a child, then disappeared.  We corresponded with a descendent of his, but have l
2 CONC ost touch.  Will try to find more information later.
2 CONT
2 CONT The point of all this is that Phoebe Lothrop Merrill couldn't have been married to William Howard Marr, because she was having children with Samuel in Cramahe, Northumberland Co., Ontario at that time.  The daughter, Phoebe, could
2 CONC n't have married him in 1830 and had a child, Chloe, in 1832, because she wasn't born until about 1845.  
2 CONT
2 CONT I think that this Phoebe was related to the Northumberland County Merrills, but I'm not sure how.  The name of Phoebe and William's daughter, might have been an honorific for Chloe Ann (nee Merry) Merrill, wife of Roger Merrill (t
2 CONC hey were parents to Solomon, b. abt. 1814, and Samuel, b. about 1801).  I think that it's more likely there was a daughter, Phoebe, related through Solomon's line, but I don't know how yet.  Solomon and wife Maria (nee Lapp) Merri
2 CONC ll had daughters that ended up in Leila Valentine Oberto's correspondence:  Daw and Burkell.  These same names show up in the correspondence of Connie McCann Wilkes, a relative by marriage, and a meticulous researcher as well (as 
2 CONC Leila and Judy Oberto).  
2 CONT
2 CONT This is a puzzle, one I hope we can solve.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Nancy Merrill Sayed, January 5, 2011
2 CONT
2 CONT Also, I thought that the Michael O'Brien in the 1870 census with the Creightons (James and Mary Ann, parents of Lillian) might have been the one that Phoebe ended up marrying.  Maybe so, but if so, she's not mentioned here (wherea
2 CONC s it's fairly certain that it's the same Michael as in the 1870 census with the Creightons):
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael O'brien.—One of the representative men of the northern part of the Lower Peninsula of the state of Michigan is well known and highly honored citizen of Alpena, where he has maintained his home for forty-two years and where
2 CONC  he is engaged in the practice of law, besides being identified with various important lines of business enterprise. He has assisted as one of the builders of Alpena, of which city he has served as mayor, and at all times has cont
2 CONC ributed his share to the material and civic progress of this thriving city. Mr. O'Brien has triumphed over adverse conditions and pressed forward to the goal of worthy success. He is a self-made man, and integrity and honor have c
2 CONC haracterized him in all the relations of life, so that he well merits the esteem so freely accorded him in the community in which he has so long made his home and in which he is a prominent and influential citizen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael O'Brien well exemplifies the sterling traits of the race from which he is sprung, and his alert mentality, mature judgment and keen business sagacity are on a parity with his genial and whole-souled personality. He was bor
2 CONC n on a farm near Belleville, province of Ontario, Canada, on the 18th of September, 1852, and is the eldest son in a family of three sons and three daughters born to Patrick and Catherine (Fitzgerald) O'Brien, both of whom were na
2 CONC tives of county Limerick, Ireland, where the former was born in the year 1821 and the latter in 1833. The parents were young at the time of coming to America; the father came in 1846 and the mother in 1848, and their marriage was 
2 CONC solemnized at Belleville, Ontario, on the 20th of August, 1849. The father died on the 14th of March, 1869, and the mother long survived him, having been summoned to eternal rest on the 21st of May, 1910, in the city of Alpena. Bo
2 CONC th were devout members of the Catholic church, and of their children two sons and three daughters are now living. Patrick O 'Brien was reared and educated in his native land and in 1846 he came to America. He bought a piece of lan
2 CONC d in the county of Hastings and soon became identified with agriculture and, being a cabinet maker, also worked at his trade near Belleville, Ontario, until about the year 1855, when he removed with his family to the city of Detro
2 CONC it, Michigan; later he entered the employ of the Great Western Railroad Company, which necessitated his removal to Windsor, and in the service of that company he continued for some time. Later on he became connected with the post 
2 CONC office department and he continued to reside in Windsor until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael O'Brien, whose name initiates this review, gained his early educational discipline in the Catholic parochial schools of Windsor, Ontario, and supplemented this by a course in the old Bryant & Stratton Business College in t
2 CONC he city of Detroit. At Windsor he learned the trade of shoe-making, in which he became a skilled workman and in 1868, at the age of sixteen years, he began working as a journeyman at his trade, in which connection he was employed 
2 CONC first at Trenton and later at Lexington, Michigan. He was not yet seventeen years of age at the time of his father's death, and his widowed mother was left with the care of five children besides Michael, the eldest son. The latter
2 CONC  thus became the virtual head of the family, and beyond the bare necessities of life for himself he devoted his earnings to the support of his mother and children.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 28th of July, 1869, Mr. O'Brien came to Alpena, which was then a small lumbering town, and here he secured work at his trade by entering the employ of Murdoch McLeod, and when that gentleman went out of business he went to 
2 CONC work for the late John W. Creighton. That he had made good use of his earlier educational advantages was shown soon after he established his home at Alpena. He attended night school three years, after which he passed the required 
2 CONC examination and proved himself eligible for pedagogic honors. In 1872 he brought his mother and the other children to Alpena and he continued to support the family until the younger children were able to do for themselves. No son 
2 CONC ever accorded more earnest and devoted filial solicitude than did he, and his noble old mother never failed to mark her appreciation of his fidelity during these earlier years of vicissitude.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1875 Mr. O'Brien engaged in the manufacturing and retail boot and shoe business on his own account and he was on the road to success when disaster overtook him, February 21, 1876, in that his store and stock were destroyed by f
2 CONC ire with other stores, with the result that he was compelled to resume the work of his trade and again start at the foot of the ladder. In the meantime he had secured firm vantage ground in the confidence and good will of the comm
2 CONC unity and his personal popularity was significantly shown by his election as alderman in April, 1880, and in November of the same year he was also elected to the office of county clerk of Alpena county, and through successive reel
2 CONC ections he served four consecutive terms in this important office, a total of eight years, and the administration of the office was careful, systematic and thorough. While incumbent of this position Mr. O 'Brien began the study of
2 CONC  law and was admitted to the bar on the 31st of December, 1887.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1889 Mr. O'Brien purchased the law and abstract business of the late Joseph B. Newton and entered into a professional and business partnership with William T. Sleator, who was then in the abstract business, and with whom he con
2 CONC tinued to be associated for ten years. Since July 1, 1899, his law and business partner has been Hon. James Francis, a former representative of Alpena county in the state legislature. They control a large and representative law pr
2 CONC actice and also conduct a general real-estate and loan business. In 1890 Mr. O'Brien was elected mayor of Alpena and he gave a progressive administration of the affairs of the municipal government. Within his regime, which compris
2 CONC ed three terms of two years each, many important public improvements were effected in the city, including the erection of the present splendid highschool building. As mayor of the city he was the prime factor in securing the const
2 CONC ruction of the first big brick sewer within the municipal limits. This is two miles in length, flushes itself automatically and affords effective drainage for the whole south side of the city, besides which it is the only sewer of
2 CONC  the kind in the entire state. Everything that has tended to conserve the best material and civic interests of his home city has received the earnest support of Mr. O'Brien, and he has been identified actively with all important m
2 CONC ovements and enterprises along this line. He was the first president of the Alpena Business Men's Association, and is a director of the Alpena Chamber of Commerce, of which he was elected the first president, an office which he he
2 CONC ld for two years. He is also a stockholder and director of the Alpena County Savings Bank. He is the secretary and a director of the Alpena Land Improvement Company, and is president of the Alpena Roller Flour Mills, incorporated 
2 CONC in 1899. His various interests, professional, business and civic, place insistent demands upon his time and attention, but he has found ample opportunity for effective service in behalf of the cause of the Democratic party, of who
2 CONC se policies and principles he is an ardent advocate. In 1902 he was a candidate for Congress, his opponent being Colonel Loud. He has also served as chairman of the county, city, judiciary and of the legislative committees and has
2 CONC  been a member of the committee for the Tenth congressional district.
2 CONT
2 CONT Both Mr. O'Brien and his wife are members of the Catholic church and are active and liberal in the support of the various departments of work in the parish with which they are identified, that of St. Bernard's church. He is identi
2 CONC fied with the Catholic Mutual Benefit Association, the Ancient Order of Hibernians and the Benevolent and Protective Order of Elks.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 28th of November, 1882, was solemnized the marriage of Mr. O'Brien to Miss Mary Ann Coppinger, who was also born at Belleville, province of Ontario, Canada, and who is a daughter of the late Mark Coppinger.
2 CONT
