2 CONT ********************************************************* From Complete History of Alpena County, 1876:
2 CONT
2 CONT At the beginning, lots of info about DD Oliver, possible relative of Albert's first wife, Ellen Oliver.
2 CONT * Mr. E.K. Potter's business was to scale and mark the logs at the landing, and he thus had the honor of scaling the first log, as well as that of measuring the first cargo of lumber that left Alpena, which was in the latter part 
2 CONC of the summer of 1859. The honor of cutting the first log belongs to Mr. Sam'l Boggs, while that of drawing it is claimed by three different parties, William Stevens, Albert Merrill, and W. Steples. Henry Doyle had a hand in sawin
2 CONC g the first log. - The schooner Meridian, Captain Flood, carried the first cargo of lumber from Alpena.
2 CONT * The Fremont fever being over, the people of Alpena wisely determined to change the name of the place from Fremont to Alpena. This was accomplished by the following act of Legislature: AN ACT to change the name of the village of 
2 CONC Fremont in the County of Alpena. SECTION 1. The People of the State of Michigan enact, That the name of village of Fremont, in the county of Alpena, State of Michigan, be and the same is hereby changed to Alpena. SEC. 2. This act 
2 CONC shall take effect immediately. Approved February 29th, 1859.
2 CONT * The ALPENA WEEKLY ARGUS of May 31, 1876, thus speaks of the contrast between the state of affairs eighteen years ago and at present: 
2 CONT
2 CONT What a difference between between our mail carrying facilities now and eighteen years ago. In 1858 it was all chance as to the receiving of mail, and in the winter season it was conveyed by Indians with dogs, while in summer it wa
2 CONC s received occasionally - just as some of the citizens visited Bay City - and was brought up by sail-boats. But what a change? Now we are supplied with a daily mail both summer and winter, - in winter by stage, and during the summ
2 CONC er season by a line of steamers which ply between Alpena and Bay City. The population of the county was but a few souls, and could be called, by naming each individual, in the space of five minutes, yet now the inhabitants of the 
2 CONC city and county will reach about eight thousand, and we may say that all have profited by their settlement in this locality.  (Albert and Mary were postmaster and postmistress)
2 CONT * At the spring election of 1866 one hundred and nineteen votes were cast, and during the same year three shingle mills were erected, one of which, built by Thompson & Co., near Campbell & Potters saw mill, has an estimated capaci
2 CONC ty of 10,000,000 shingles per year. - At the fall election in November, 220 votes were cast. (could this Thompson be related to Mary?)
2 CONT * J.W. Creighton, manufacturer and dealer of boots and shoes, Fine Custom Work a Specialty, Second Street, Alpena (from business directory at below url--could this be family of Grandpa Merrill's first wife, Lillian Creighton/Craig
2 CONC head?) http://www.geocities.com/heartland/acres/1947/completehistoryofalpenaco.html
2 CONT ********************************************************* Mary Thompson was his second or third spouse.  They married April 6, 1876.  Albert died of tuberculosis abt. March 11, 1880 in Alpena, Alpena Co., MI.
2 CONT
2 CONT Long Rapids & Vicinity, compiled by Ruth Ann Cochrane.  Lachine, MI  1987
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 4 By 1871 Albert Merrill had owned a sawmill near the present village.  The settlement around his mill was known as Merrillsville.  In 1873 when the founders were looking for a name for the township and post office, they could 
2 CONC not call it Merrillsville because there already was a Merrillsville in St. Clair County, Michigan.  
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 7 Early settlers who settled near the present Village of Long Rapids, also known as the burning, were…Albert Merrill...
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 9
2 CONT
2 CONT John Farnell came to Long Rapids during the early 1870’s.  When he first arrived in Long Rapids, he hired to press hay for Albert Merrill at $26.00 a month.  They used a hand press and by working hard could press four tons of hay 
2 CONC daily.  Later he worked in the woods and purchased his own farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 10 John Butler came to Alpena from Huron County, Ontario, Canada in November of 1869.  He worked for Churchill and Harrington, Minor Lumber Company, the Boom Company, Albert Pack, Holmes and Wright, Gaffney and Rayburn.  He wor
2 CONC ked in sawmills and on river drives.  He moved to Long Rapids in the late 1879’s and later purchased Albert Merrill’s farm by Sauls and Gohl Roads.  He was a very successful farmer and an ardent worker in fraternal orders and poli
2 CONC tical affairs.
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 30 Many shingle mills were in operation during the Lumbering Era in the area. Among the ones owning and operating mills were Albert Merrill.
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 49 1903 Patron’s Directory of Alpena County, Michigan, Long Rapids.  Merrill, Frank F.  Farmer Long Rapids Sec. 33
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 59 Long Rapids Township was served by several schools throughout the years.  Schools which were in Long Rapids Township include the Merrill (Beaty) School…
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1887, the Public Schools of Long Rapids Township purchased land on the corner of Long Rapids and Gohl Roads for the purpose of building the Merrill (Beatty) School.  The school was named for Albert Merrill who owned an
2 CONC d operated a sawmill in the area during the 1870’s.  (cont. on page 60) At one time Mr. Merrill owned the property which was later sold to the township for school purposes.  Teachers who taught at the Merrill School include Ethel 
2 CONC Burdette Wright, Margaret Mulvena, Eva Den and Maggie Lou Small.
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 66 Three pictures of Merrill School with children and teachers.
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 75 Others owning and operating stores in the vicinity in 1883 were Alonzo McBride and Mary Merrill.  The stores supplied the items needed by the lumbermen and farmers.
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 78 Others owning stores in the vicinity during the late 1800’s were Mrs. M. LeRoy (formerly Mrs. Albert Merrill, later married to Martin LeRoy), and G.W. Brown.  Mrs. LeRoy ran a small dry good store.
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 85 1883 Long Rapids Directory Merrill, Mary (in bold) General Store 1889 Long Rapids Directory LeRoy, Mrs. M., dry goods 1891 Long Rapids Directory LeRoy, Mrs. M., dry goods
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 87 Postmasters:  Albert Merrill Appointment date:  April 13, 1877 (After his death in 1881, his wife Mary, served as postmistress)
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert Merrill 39y married Mary Thompson 30y (later Mrs. Martin LeRoy) 6 Apr 1876; occupation--farmer; Alpena.  L. A. P., # 42.  Albert’s first wife was Ellen (Oliver) who died 20 Sep 1876 {She actually died 20 Sep 1875 according 
2 CONC to GENDIS--nms}
2 CONT
2 CONT Children in Alpena Co. Birth index Merrill, Minnie p. 1 (Were John and Minnie twins, or simply born the same year?) Merrill, John p. 1 Merrill, Frank p. 16 (note:  born 12 Dec 1874--see baptismal record below for Frank Forest) Mer
2 CONC rill, William Albert p. 46 (note:  born June 12, 1876--see birth record in notes for Wm. Albert)
2 CONT
2 CONT Grace Lutheran Church Records, Alpena, MI Married by Rev. John Evans at Long Rapids, MI
2 CONT #4 Martin LeRoy and Mrs. Mary Merrill on Wednesday, March 7, 1883
2 CONT
2 CONT Burials--Grace Lutheran Church Records, Long Rapids Tuesday, Oct. 10, 1916 Mrs. Wm. A. Merrill (Lillian Craighead, first wife--nms) Died Oct. 6, 1916.  Burial--Alpena
2 CONT
2 CONT Record of Baptisms--Grace Lutheran Church 1888 Jan 1, Frank Forest Merrill born Dec. 12, 1874
2 CONT
2 CONT 1901 Jan 13 Emily Nyma Merrill Naverly Twp. Nov. 30, 1900--Sydney and Hannah Merrill, the mother. (don't know who this Sydney is, or if he's related--nms)
2 CONT
2 CONT 1902 Jun 17 Cora Mable (Wright) Merrill b. Kansas 23 Jun 1880.  Res- Wellington & Elizabeth Sanborn, Sponsors- Parents
2 CONT
2 CONT 1903 Dec Raymond John Merrill Long Rapids, 12 Jun 1903.  Parents; Frank Forest Merrill and Cora Mable Wright.  Sponsors: parents and Mrs. Mary LeRoy
2 CONT
2 CONT Evergreen Cemetery, Alpena, Alpena, MI--Lot 14-58 Martin LeRoy 12 Apr 1841-30 Oct 1928 Mary LeRoy 27 Dec 1842-12 Dec 1917
2 CONT
2 CONT Grantee-Grantor Index, Alpena, Alpena, MI Albert Merril, grantee/State of MI grantor L. 2, P. 33, Sec. 32.  Town 32, Range 6 (no date for the above--nms)
2 CONT
2 CONT Hope this is of some help. Jolene Kelly Pillars Alpena Co. Lookup <http://www.rootsweb.com/~mialpena/lookup.htm>
2 CONT
2 CONT Feel free to get back with me if you would like me to check the censuses for Alpena and or the birth and death records for the Merrill Family
2 CONT
2 CONT (copy of information sent by Jolene Kelly Pillars to Nancy Merrill Sayed--in smaller text for convenience.  Copied 2/14/02)
2 CONT ************************************************** Excerpt from email: Subj:	 Re: Michigan and Canadian Merrills Date:	1/9/2002 3:18:11 PM Central Standard Time From:	hjmerril@eatel.net (howard merrill) To:	Steve@Merrill.org CC:	N
2 CONC Sayed6266@aol.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Nancy:
2 CONT
2 CONT We recently obtained a 56 CD set of the 1880 census and we checked it for Albert [even though he shouldn't be listed if the Mar 11 1880 death date is correct]. We found him in Long Rapids and have pasted the record below. You prob
2 CONC ably already have this data, but thought we'd send it just in case you didn't.
2 CONT
2 CONT Census Place: Long Rapids, Alpena, Michigan
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: FHL Film 1254570  National Archives Film T9-0570     Page 519A
2 CONT
2 CONT Name  Relation Marital Status Gender Race Age Birthplace Occupation Father's Birthplace Mother's Birthplace  Albert MERRILL Self M M W 45 CAN Occ: Farmer Fa: CAN Mo: CAN Mary MERRILL Wife F M W 35 ENG Occ: Keeps House Fa: SCOT Mo:
2 CONC  SCOT Minnie MERRILL Dau F S W 9 MI Occ: At School Fa: CAN Mo: CAN Frank MERRILL Son M S W 6 MI Occ: At School Fa: CAN Mo: CAN William MERRILL Son M S W 3 MI   Fa: CAN Mo: ENG John MERRILL Son M S W 1 MI   Fa: CAN Mo: ENG Robert H
2 CONC ICKS Other M S W 26 CAN Occ: Works On Farm Fa: CAN Mo: SCOT Elizabeth TEETS Other F S W 14 CAN Occ: Servant Fa: NY Mo: IRE
2 CONT ******************************************************** September 14, 1876 Albert Merrill, along with others, elected to executive board of Agricultural Society. (p. 202, Title: History of the Lake Huron shore. With illustrations
2 CONC  and biographical sketches of some of its prominent men and pioneers.  Collection: Michigan County Histories and Atlases http://quod.lib.umich.edu/cgi/t/text/pageviewer-idx?c=micounty&cc=micounty&idno=bad0770.0001.001&q1=Merrill&v
2 CONC iew=image&seq=246&size=200
2 CONT ******************************************************** MERRILL, ALBERT 						
2 CONT  						 	Date of death:	11-Mar-1880					 	Ledger Page:	24					 	Record Number:	487					 	Place of death:	Long Rapids					 	County of Death:	Alpena					 	Sex:	Male					 	Race:	WHITE					 	Marital Status:	Married					 	Age:	44 years
2 CONC  					 	Cause of Death:	CONSUMPTION					 	Birthplace:	CANADA					 	Occupation:	FARMER					 	Father's Name:	Merrill, SAMUEL					 	Father's Residence:	CANADA					 	Mother's Name:	MERRILL, First name not recorded					 	Mother's Resid
2 CONC ence:	CANADA					 	Date of record:	1-Jul-1881 http://www.mdch.state.mi.us/gendisx/scripts/individual.idc?UniqueID=228785
2 CONT *********************************************************** Timeline for Albert Forest looks like this:
2 CONT
2 CONT Born:  Abt 1836 in Northumberland County, Ontario (1852 census shows him 15 and still living at 
2 CONT          home) By:     January 30, 1859 living in Fremont (Alpena, MI?) By:     Summer 1859, one of three claiming honor of  By:     Abt. 1860, married (to whom?) and living in Saginaw, Michigan? (150 miles from Alpena to Saginaw 
2 CONC by land, but probably much easier on the lake by boat) By      May 1868, Ida born if the Albert as father on death certificate was our Albert (Alpena) By:     1869, was living in Alpena.  His child, Ida O. Merrill, died August 22 
2 CONC of this year, at 1 year and       
2 CONT           3  months (if this is their certificate--who was Eliza, though? By:     1870 living in Long Rapids Twp., Alpena, with Ellen (1870 census); no children By:     1871 Albert Merrill owned a sawmill near the village of Merri
2 CONC llsville (now Long Rapids), MI By:     18 Apr 1871 daughter Minnie born, son John born By:     Abt 1874 son Frank Forest born By:     Sept 1875 Ellen dies By:     April 6, 1876 marries Mary Thompson By:     June 1876 William Alber
2 CONC t born (3 years old in 1880 census, but when was census 
2 CONT          taken?--okay, it was taken on 1 Jun 1880, which would make him 3 years old)
2 CONT          Also told me his 21st birthday was spent during Spanish-American War--was at a farmhouse 
2 CONT          and a pretty young girl gave him a glass of water from a well), which would have put him born 
2 CONT          in 1877.  Different censuses give different ages and birth years. By:     John born about 1879--he is one year old in the 1880 census By:     11 Mar 1880, death of Albert Forest Merrill according to Gendis
2 CONT ******************************* Additional information:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1870 Alpena County, Michigan Mortality Schedule Persons who died during the year ending 1st June 1870 in Alpena 228 Thompson, George 1/12 M W MI   December  Diarrhea  280 Merrick, Ida 1 F W MI * * August (I think this is our littl
2 CONC e Ida, misspelled last name) http://www.rootsweb.com/~mialpena/BMD/1870.htm
2 CONT
