1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Ellen Minott Sherman'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/98793535/ellen-minott-sherman Find A Grave Memorial ID # 98793535]
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father’s ancestry included a long line of ministers and lawyers, including her great-uncle founding father Roger Sherman. Her father, Henry Sherman, was a Yale graduate and a noted lawyer, judge and author. Her maternal grandf
2 CONC ather, Michael Burnham, was the publisher of the New York Evening Post. The Post was founded in 1801, by founding father Alexander Hamilton, and was a respected prior to its 19th century tabloid format.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Ellen was 11 years old, her father took a job with the US Treasury Department and moved the family to Washington. In 1867, Ellen was enrolled at Vassar College in Poughkeepsie, New York. Vassar had been founded just six years
2 CONC  earlier and was the first degree-granting institution of higher education for women in the United States. With Ellen’s college graduation also came her confirmation at Epiphany Church. Ellen’s parents are both listed as Epiphany 
2 CONC communicants at the time. It comes as no surprise that Ellen would want to spend her life enabling the education of others. As can be seen in the accompanying advertisement in the Atlantic Monthly, Ellen operated “Miss Ellen Minot
2 CONC t Sherman’s Boarding and Day School for Girls.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ellen Minott Sherman passed away February 23, 1901, age 50 and was buried from Epiphany.
2 CONT
