1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Life sketch≤p>(Ithaca Daily Journal, pg. 3, June 1st 1889)≤/p>≤p>--Mr. Timothy Hollister of South Aurora street, who has been ill for some time, is very low to-day and it is thought he cannot survive many hours. Mr. Hollister was 
2 CONC eighty-three years of age last September, and for thirty-eight years he has lived in Ithaca. He was born in Caroline and lived there until he moved to this place. A very active man until his last sickness, he was known throughout 
2 CONC the county, his business as a lumber dealer and mill owner bringing him into general acquaintance with the residents of this entire region.≤/p>≤p>-----------------≤/p>≤p>(Ithaca Daily Journal, pg. 3, June 3rd 1889)≤/p>≤p>THE LATE 
2 CONC TIMOTHY HOLLISTER≤/p>≤p>The illness of Mr. Timothy Hollister, to which reference was made in the Journal last week, resulted fatally on Saturday evening. The sad event was not unexpected and the old gentleman's family were gathere
2 CONC d around the bedside when he passed away.≤/p>≤p>The deceased was the son of Isaac Hollister and Ruth Winchell, and was born in Olive, Ulster county, N.Y., September 28th 1805. When a child he removed with his parents to the town o
2 CONC f Caroline, this county, where he resided until coming to Ithaca about 38 years ago. ≤/p>≤p>Mr. Hollister was engaged in building the first railroad that ran into this place. During the greater part of his life he was interested i
2 CONC n the lumber business, and when stricken by his fatal illness a few weeks ago was still at his advanced age, actively engaged in his customary work.≤/p>≤p>Mr. Hollister was a man of large physique and was a stiking type of the har
2 CONC dy, unostentatious, honest, industrious pioneers to whom this present generation are indebted for the opening and development of this region. Although not a member of any church he early professed a belief in Christianity and has 
2 CONC conscientiously endeavored to live a true Christian life. ≤/p>≤p>Kind and approachable at all times, he was loved by all who know him intimately and will be greatly missed by many outside the immediate family.≤/p>≤p>He was married
2 CONC  in 1835 to Harriet Coon who, with six children, survives to mourn his loss. His surviving children are Mrs. E.M. Latta, Harriet Hollister, Mrs. P.A. Osborn, Holmes Hollister, Mrs. John J. Trench and Mrs. Lemi N. Howe.≤/p>≤p>The f
2 CONC uneral services will occur to-morrow, Tuesday afternoon at 2 o'clock. Interment in the family plot at East Lawn cemetery.≤/p>
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Alternate name:≤p>Timothy≤/p>
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
