1 OCCU Married Thomas Jefferson Patten in Salt Lake City, Utah, in 1853
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The above photo was taken in 1885 and was found on Ancestry.com.  Joanna Hollister was born in Caroline, New York March 18, 1833, to John Hollister and Lavina or Vina Clearwater.  When an infant, her parents moved 
2 CONC to Kirtland, Ohio, where they joined the LDS church and helped in the building and dedication of the Kirtland Temple.   At the age of 3, Joanna's family moved to Missouri where they suffered persecutions and fled to Quincy, Illino
2 CONC is.  The climax to the Missouri persecutions was the massacre at Hauns Mill.  Joanna's parents were at Hauns Mill the day after the massacre.  They were driven from their own homes by the mob which was carrying out an exterminatio
2 CONC n order from Governor Wilburn W. Boggs.  Joanna's father lay out in the woods in the cold secreting himself form the mob;  he died from this exposure leaving Vina alone with 8 sick children.  They were without a home living in a w
2 CONC agon, all sick while Vina cared for them and drove the team back to friends in Ohio.  As soon they could, they moved to Nauvoo, Illinois, where Joanna was baptized in 1844.  They stayed there until they were driven out of the city
2 CONC  after a severe battle and being disarmed into the winter weather.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The women were searched for arms while camped on the banks of the Mississippi river with nothing to their name.  The mob fired at them, but failed to injure anyone.  They came west with the Church through the trackless wilderness,
2 CONC  suffering the privations and trials.  Of this time she writes: " The Lord sent quails which supplied our wants."  The struggle to get the family to Utah lasted from 1847 to 1852 when they finally arrived in Salt Lake City, Joanna
2 CONC  was 19 years old.  
2 CONT
2 CONT They were sent with a group of saints to help settle Provo and this is where Joanna met Thomas Jefferson Patten to whom she was married April 25, 1853 in Provo, Utah.  Their honeymoon was a perilous adventure.  The day after they 
2 CONC were married they were sent to Manti to take part in the Indian Walker War.  They lived in a wagon and the Indians, bitter and hostile, stole their cattle and most of their possessions and they were forced to live in forts.  At th
2 CONC e close of the war, they returned to their homes, but their crops were entirely lost when eaten by the grasshoppers.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1856 they were called on a special colonization mission to Carson Valley, Nevada for a year.  As they returned, news reached them of Johnson's army invading Utah and met this crisis with courage and strength.  Joanna and her hu
2 CONC sband took a homestead on Provo bench-the first family to settle there among their family and friends ridicule and protest.  They tore the sagebrush by hand and prepared the ground for alfalfa the crop they were told would never g
2 CONC row in that section.  In order to get the necessary water, they dug by hand a ditch from the mouth of Provo Canyon to their home-a great distance.  They built a board house 15' x 15' with a huge fireplace; where the cooking was do
2 CONC ne.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The coyotes were numerous and stole chickens, calves and pigs whenever they could.  Through all the years of struggle, Joanna's spirit never faltered.  They felt strongly about social development and education opportunities so the
2 CONC y donated a part of their land for the 1st school and church.  She was a great influence and was known for her ability as an organizer.  She was active in the woman's suffrage and helped make Utah one of the first states to gain s
2 CONC tatehood with suffrage.  She was also famous for her ministry to the sick with invaluable knowledge of remedies made from native herbs.  She died December 2, 1916, in Provo, Utah.  They had 10 children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography obtained from Daughters of the Utah Pioneers (edited)
2 CONT
2 CONT Transcribed by Jocelyn Jones (#47487085)
2 CONT
2 CONT ___________________________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Joanna's OBITUARY "RESOLUTIONS OF RESPECT"
2 CONT
2 CONT To the memory of Sister Joanna Hollister Patten who departed this life on December 2nd, 1916 at her home on Provo Bench.  Whereas, We the members of the Daughters of the Utah Pioneers, of the Provo Chapter, feel to mourn the loss 
2 CONC of our sister, and to sympathize with her bereaved family and friends, and being desirous of expressing our respect for her memory and virtues, she being a faithful member of our association, and whereas we have appreciated and en
2 CONC joyed her companionship and know that she has borne her humble but ferverent testimony concerning the Temple and Heavenly things and was firmly attached to the Gospel of Jesus Christ.
2 CONT
2 CONT Therefore, Be it Resolved, that, as God has seen fit in His providence to remove our sister from our midst, we feel to acknowledge His will, to emulate her example, and affectionately cherish her memory;
2 CONT
2 CONT Resolved, that copies of these resolutions be spread upon the records of our association, presented to the family, and that they be published in the Prove papers.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Let us do our duty ever. That, when our time is at hand. We may meet and greet each member in that bright eternal land."
2 CONT
2 CONT -Gertrude Page,
2 CONT
2 CONT -Eunice Harris
2 CONT
2 CONT -Lydia Billings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Committee
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary 1 February 1917 Provo Daily Herald pg.1
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT JOANNA HOLLISTER PATTEN WAS LAID TO REST WED.:
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral services for Mrs. Joanna Hollister Patten, held yesterday in the Timpanogas ward house were attended by a large congregation of friends and relatives.  The opening prayer was offered by Elder Jorgen Hansen. Elder J. E.
2 CONC  Booth was the first speaker. He testified to the many good qualities of the departed and spoke of the trials she had endured as an early member of the church and a pioneer.  Mrs. Rose Young Stewart, representing the Utah Daughter
2 CONC s of the Pioneers, read a sketch of Mrs Patten's life and Mrs. Delia I. Booth representing the Stake Relief Society, spoke of the worthy connection of Mrs. Patten with the Relief Society. Bishop Otis L. Terry offered consolation t
2 CONC o the many relatives.  The music was by the ward choir.  Elder Theodore Parley offered the benediction.  A profusion of floral offerings evidenced the love and esteem of many friends.  Six of Mrs. Patten's grandsons were the pall 
2 CONC bearers.
2 CONT
2 CONT The grave was dedicated by Elder Farley.  7 December 1916 Provo Daily Herald pg.4 
2 CONT
