1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===DOCTOR OLIVER ALBERT JUDSON===
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage===
2 CONT
2 CONT Doctor Oliver Albert Judson married  Elizabeth Boyce on 19 April 1866, in the District of Columbia, in the United States of America. [https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:XL8G-831 District of Columbia Marriages]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children===
2 CONT
2 CONT # https://www.geni.com/people/Oliver-Judson/6000000013218130114?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1476112061578&through=6000000013218130114
2 CONT # https://www.geni.com/people/Doctor-Charles-Judson/6000000045365903988?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1476112338157&through=6000000013218161086
2 CONT # https://www.geni.com/people/Mary-Judson/6000000048109011823?from_flash=1&fsession_id=1476112388069&through=6000000013218161086
2 CONT
2 CONT irth: 	Sep. 28, 1830 Connecticut, USA Death: 	Mar. 30, 1898 Philadelphia Philadelphia County Pennsylvania, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Civil War Union Army Surgeon. He was commissioned as Surgeon, United States Volunteers on October 5, 1861. During his service during the war he commanded the Carver United States Army General Hospital, which was located on 14th St
2 CONC reet West, Washington, D.C. He was brevetted Colonel, United States Volunteers on March 13, 1865 for faithful and meritorious services during the war and was honorably mustered out on November 17, 1865.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philadelphia Inquirer Friday, April 1, 1898 Death of Dr. Oliver A. Judson Dr. Oliver Albert Judson, who has been in poor health since the close of the war, died on Wednesday at his home, 2010 DeLancey Place. He was 69 years old, w
2 CONC as the son of Rev. Albert Judson, D.D., a Presbyterian clergyman and graduated from the Jefferson Hospital in 1851. Dr. Judson was a member of the Military Order of the Loyal Legion, the Sons of the Revolution, the Rittenhouse Clu
2 CONC b, the Pennsylvania Historical Society, the Reform Club, the Social Art Club and other organizations. 
2 CONT
