1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.encyclopediavirginia.org/Burnham_Horace_Blois_1824-1894
2 CONT
2 CONT Horace B. Burnham was an officer in the U.S. Army during the American Civil War (1861–1865); chief judge advocate of Military District One, the army unit that administered Virginia during Reconstruction; judge of the Richmond City
2 CONC  Hustings Court (1867–1869); and justice of the Virginia Supreme Court of Appeals (1869–1870). Burnham grew up in Pennsylvania, where he studied and practiced law before the war. He presided over the Supreme Court of Appeals as Vi
2 CONC rginia transitioned back to civilian rule. The legitimacy of the court, and his position on it, was called into question early in 1870, when Congress passed an act ending Reconstruction in Virginia and implementing a new state con
2 CONC stitution under which the General Assembly would select new judges. The military court prevailed against a legal challenge, but by then Burnham had been thrown out of the body. He remained a military justice in courts across the c
2 CONC ountry until 1888, when he retired to his Henrico County estate. He died in 1894 and was buried in Arlington National Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=18757372&amp;ref=acom
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.encyclopediavirginia.org/Burnham_Horace_Blois_1824-1894
2 CONT
2 CONT Horace B. Burnham was an officer in the U.S. Army during the American Civil War (1861–1865); chief judge advocate of Military District One, the army unit that administered Virginia during Reconstruction; judge of the Richmond City
2 CONC  Hustings Court (1867–1869); and justice of the Virginia Supreme Court of Appeals (1869–1870). Burnham grew up in Pennsylvania, where he studied and practiced law before the war. He presided over the Supreme Court of Appeals as Vi
2 CONC rginia transitioned back to civilian rule. The legitimacy of the court, and his position on it, was called into question early in 1870, when Congress passed an act ending Reconstruction in Virginia and implementing a new state con
2 CONC stitution under which the General Assembly would select new judges. The military court prevailed against a legal challenge, but by then Burnham had been thrown out of the body. He remained a military justice in courts across the c
2 CONC ountry until 1888, when he retired to his Henrico County estate. He died in 1894 and was buried in Arlington National Cemetery. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Early Years
2 CONT
2 CONT Horace Blois Burnham was the son of Judson Williams Burnham and Mary Blois Burnham. He was born on September 10, 1824, probably in Columbia County, New York. The family moved to eastern Pennsylvania, where Burnham grew up and stud
2 CONC ied law. He was admitted to the bar at Wilkes-Barre on August 12, 1844. By 1848 he had married Ruth Ann Jackson. They had one son and two daughters. Burnham lived in Mauch Chunk (later Jim Thorpe), Pennsylvania, and practiced law 
2 CONC until the outbreak of the Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 26, 1861, Burnham began recruiting a regiment and on October 31 was commissioned lieutenant colonel of the 67th Pennsylvania Infantry Regiment, which was called into federal service on March 31, 1862. The unit was initiall
2 CONC y assigned guard duty in Maryland but had the majority of its personnel captured at the Second Battle of Winchester on June 15, 1863. Burnham avoided this fate, and except for an interval in New York City during which he briefly c
2 CONC ommanded a brigade putting down the draft riots in mid-July 1863, he led the two-company rump of the regiment until the captured men were exchanged and returned to duty that October. In December 1863 Burnham was detailed to Washin
2 CONC gton for service as a judge advocate, and with the expiration of his three-year term of enlistment he resigned on November 18, 1864, as lieutenant colonel of the 67th and accepted a commission as major and judge advocate, to date 
2 CONC from October 31 of that year. When his painful sciatica permitted he served on general courts-martial under the orders of the Department of War. Burnham was awarded the brevet ranks of lieutenant colonel and colonel of volunteers 
2 CONC on March 13, 1865. From May 23, 1866, until April 1867 he served in the Bureau of Military Justice, and on February 25, 1867, he was transferred to the permanent military establishment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reconstruction
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 18, 1867, Burnham was appointed chief judge advocate of Military District Number One, the army unit that administered Virginia during Reconstruction, and he moved to Richmond. On September 11, 1867, the commanding general
2 CONC  appointed him judge of the Richmond City Hustings Court to succeed William H. Lyons, who had died. Burnham presided over the Hustings Court in the trial of civil and criminal cases. A separate bench composed of the city's alderme
2 CONC n (sometimes called the Hustings Court of Magistrates) dealt with misdemeanors, issued licenses to keepers of ordinaries, and attended to similar administrative duties. The Mayor's Court dispensed summary judgments in cases involv
2 CONC ing petty misbehavior. Burnham first convened his court on September 16, 1867, with a charge to the grand jury promising that "no distinction will be tolerated which discriminates between individuals by reason of caste or color." 
2 CONC He was described at the time as small in stature, with bright blue eyes and auburn hair. Burnham attended the court faithfully, often six times a week, until the commanding general appointed him to the Virginia Supreme Court of Ap
2 CONC peals on June 9, 1869.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burnham took office six days later as one of three new members, all appointed by the general after he dismissed the sitting judges from office in compliance with a new federal law that required replacement of all Virginia and Texa
2 CONC s officials with any previous record of Confederate activity. Burnham became president of the court by election of its membership. He was not a dominant jurist. Appearing in the minority more often than his two colleagues, he wrot
2 CONC e no majority opinions and only one dissenting opinion in the eight cases the court decided while he was a member that were recorded in the official volumes of the Virginia Reports. Burnham had a brief and stormy career on the cou
2 CONC rt. He and his fellow justices, Orloff M. Dorman and Westel Willoughby, met for the first time from June 22 to 28, 1869, and adopted new rules of practice. The court met again on October 12, 1869, but decided only one case before 
2 CONC adjourning until January. In announcing the decision Burnham explained that because Virginia had adopted a new constitution and awaited only congressional approval to implement it and replace military appointees with new officials
2 CONC , it would be expedient for the court to defer further business until the next term. Newspaper reports suggested that the sudden adjournment resulted less from such questions of delicacy than from a desire to head off an effort by
2 CONC  those who doubted the court's legitimacy to seek a restraining order from the United States Supreme Court against further action by the Virginia tribunal.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Supreme Court of Appeals reconvened on January 11, 1870, but its validity was brought into dispute with passage on January 26 of an act of Congress ending Reconstruction in Virginia, restoring civilian rule, and thereby implem
2 CONC enting a new state constitution under which the General Assembly would select new judges. Critics argued that the court's proceedings were a mere nullity after the end of military rule, but it doggedly remained sitting until it co
2 CONC mpleted its business and adjourned on February 25, 1870. In March 1870 the General Assembly declared the decisions of the judges lawful and final. Later in that year the five new judges of the Supreme Court of Appeals refused to q
2 CONC uestion the quality of the three judges or the lawfulness of their decisions when they invalidated by a vote of 3–2 a provision of a law to allow some of the cases to be heard again.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burnham no doubt welcomed this judicial endorsement of the court's validity, even though he had been unceremoniously ousted from office late in the court's last session. On February 10, 1870, a member of the House of Delegates mov
2 CONC ed that the Committee for Courts of Justice report whether the judges of the court were exercising their functions lawfully. Six days later the committee reserved judgment on the status of Dorman and Willoughby but reported that B
2 CONC urnham was unquestionably not entitled to his office because under Virginia law he could not serve on a state court while holding a commission in the army. A joint resolution removing Burnham from the Court of Appeals quickly pass
2 CONC ed both houses of the General Assembly and received the governor's approval on February 22, 1870. Ironically, Burnham's removal was cited as key evidence supporting the legitimacy of the court, because in leaving his fellow milita
2 CONC ry appointees in office the assembly tacitly approved the court's functioning with judges the commanding general of the army had appointed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burnham's brief judicial experience during Reconstruction subjected him to some strong criticism and abuse. One highly prejudiced newspaper story published in 1867 declared that when he practiced law in Pennsylvania he had been a 
2 CONC "fifth-rate lawyer and first-class libertine" who "'mixed' with black women" and had escaped an adultery conviction only by bribing the wronged husband to absent himself from the trial.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the end of Reconstruction in Virginia, Burnham continued to serve until June 11, 1870, as judge advocate of the new military Department of Virginia, which encompassed four states. He was then assigned to the Department of the
2 CONC  South. Burnham served in the Department of Texas from April 24 to November 2, 1872. From November 20, 1872, to September 10, 1886, he was stationed at Omaha as judge advocate of the Department of the Platte and won promotion on J
2 CONC uly 5, 1884, to deputy judge advocate general. Burnham concluded his career in San Francisco with service in the Department of California and Military Division of the Pacific.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later Years
2 CONT
2 CONT Burnham retired from the army on September 10, 1888. He returned to Virginia and lived at Aspen Shade, the Henrico County estate he had acquired during the 1860s. The hard feelings of Reconstruction had somewhat subsided, and the 
2 CONC author of an obituary in a Richmond newspaper described him as quiet, unostentatious, and highly esteemed. Burnham died at Aspen Shade on April 10, 1894, of what was variously described as pneumonia and as neuralgia of the heart b
2 CONC rought on by a severe cold. He left an estate valued at more than $10,000 and was buried with Masonic honors in Arlington National Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT Time Line
2 CONT
2 CONT September 10, 1824 - Horace B. Burnham is born, probably in Columbia County, New York, to Judson Williams Burnham and Mary Blois Burnham.
2 CONT
2 CONT August 12, 1844 - Horace B. Burnham is admitted to the bar at Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1848 - By this date, Horace B. Burnham and Ruth Ann Jackson are married. They will have one son and two daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT July 26, 1861 - Horace B. Burnham begins to recruit a regiment to serve in the American Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT October 31, 1861 - Horace B. Burnham is commissioned lieutenant colonel of the 67th Pennsylvania Infantry Regiment.
2 CONT
2 CONT March 31, 1862 - The 67th Pennsylvania Infantry Regiment is called into federal service.
2 CONT
2 CONT June 15, 1863 - The majority of the personnel of the 67th Pennsylvania Infantry Regiment are captured at the Second Battle of Winchester.
2 CONT
2 CONT December 1863 - Horace B. Burnham is detailed to Washington for service as a judge advocate.
2 CONT
2 CONT November 18, 1864 - With the expiration of his three-year term of enlistment, Horace B. Burnham resigns as lieutenant colonel of the 67th Pennsylvania Infantry Regiment and accepts a commission as major and judge advocate, to date
2 CONC  from October 31.
2 CONT
2 CONT March 13, 1865 - Horace B. Burnham is awarded the brevet ranks of lieutenant colonel and colonel of volunteers.
2 CONT
2 CONT May 23, 1866–April 1867 - Horace B. Burnham serves in the Bureau of Military Justice.
2 CONT
2 CONT April 18, 1867 - Horace B. Burnham is appointed chief judge advocate of Military District Number One, the army unit that administers Virginia during Reconstruction. He moves to Richmond.
2 CONT
2 CONT September 11, 1867 - Horace B. Burnham succeeds William H. Lyons as judge of
2 CONT  the Richmond City Hustings Court.
2 CONT
2 CONT September 16, 1867 - Horace B. Burnham first convenes the Hustings Court with a charge to the grand jury promising, "No distinction will be tolerated which discriminates between individuals by reason of caste or color."
2 CONT
2 CONT June 9, 1869 - Horace B. Burnham is appointed to the Virginia Supreme Court of Appeals. He takes office six days later as one of the court's three new members, and becomes president.
2 CONT
2 CONT June 22–29, 1869 - Horace B. Burnham, Orloff Mather Dorman, and Westel Willoughby, judges of the Virginia Supreme Court of Appeals, meet for the first time and adopt new rules of practice.
2 CONT
2 CONT October 12, 1869 - The judges of the Virginia Supreme Court of Appeals meet, but decide only one case before adjourning until January.
2 CONT
2 CONT January 11, 1870 - The Virginia Supreme Court of Appeals reconvenes.
2 CONT
2 CONT January 26, 1870 - An act of Congress ends Reconstruction in Virginia, readmitting Virginia into the United States and restoring civilian rule.
2 CONT
2 CONT February 22, 1870 - A joint resolution removing Horace B. Burnham from the Virginia Supreme Court of Appeals receives the governor's approval.
2 CONT
2 CONT June 11, 1870 - Horace B. Burnham serves as judge advocate of the new military Department of Virginia until this date.
2 CONT
2 CONT April 24–November 2, 1872 - Horace B. Burnham serves as judge advocate in the Department of Texas.
2 CONT
2 CONT November 20, 1872–September 10, 1886 - Horace B. Burnham is stationed at Omaha as judge advocate of the Department of the Platte.
2 CONT
2 CONT July 5, 1884 - Horace B. Burnham, stationed at Omaha as judge advocate of the Department of the Platte, is promoted to deputy judge advocate general.
2 CONT
2 CONT September 10, 1888 - Horace B. Burnham retires from the U.S. Army.
2 CONT
2 CONT April 10, 1894 - Horace B. Burnham dies at Aspen Shade, his estate in Henrico County, of what is variously described as pneumonia and as neuralgia of the heart. He is buried with Masonic honors in Arlington National Cemetery.
