1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT - lists his date of birth as 7/7/1845 and his death as 7/7/1926.  and list his date of birth as 1845 and his death as 1926.
2 CONT
2 CONT EDUCATION:  "He [Franklin] attended the old brick school over here at the corner where James Mac Danold's house now stands" [in 1934]  (predecessor to the Mary A. Callen School at the corner of Middle Street and Pine Street, Brist
2 CONC ol, CT)
2 CONT
2 CONT CHURCH MEMBERSHIP:  Joined the First Congregational Church, Bristol, CT 1871 page 105, ,,.  According to Franklin Ensign Terry and his wife Isabella (Perry) Terry were admitted 1/1/1875 by Confession of  Faith. Franklin Ensign Ter
2 CONC ry was a member of the Bristol Congregational Society Committee in 1889.
2 CONT "He sang in the Choir and at times  was a solist." "At one time the choir was in the back gallery of the church. I can remember it." 
2 CONT
2 CONT OCCUPATION:  E.N. Welch Co. (Clock shops), Forestville, CT, Worked at E.N.W. Co. as of 1891;  Was listed as being a mechanic in1893 and in 1895; Worked at N. D. Bell, Co., Bristol, CT as of 1896; Sessions Co. ; President of The Fl
2 CONC etcher-Terry Co., Inc.
2 CONT "Franklin E. Terry was a clockmaker at Bristol, CT.  He worked in the clock shops of the E. N. Welch Mfg. Co.  After that firm was taken over by the Sessions family in 1903, Franklin was offered the position of superintendent of t
2 CONC he movement department at the Sessions Clock Company.  Later in life he went into business with his son-in-law, Fred S. Fletcher, and became {Assistant Manager May 1, 1903} and  the President of the Fletcher-Terry Company,  which 
2 CONC manufactured glass cutters and light hardware." (Nov 20, 1911at the first stockholders meeting the following officers were elected: Franklin E. Terry,  President; Isabelle (Terry) Fletcher, Vice President; Fred S. Fletcher, Secret
2 CONC ary and Treasurer)
2 CONT "At the time I [Isabelle] was married June 2, 1897, Papa [Franklin] was working in Winstead, CT in the clock shop. His father [Ralph] has worked there years before and had been foreman of the Movement Departmant. Emerson [Ralph's 
2 CONC brother and son of Samuel Terry] also worked there at the same time as his father [Ralph]. [Emerson] at the age of sixteen." [1854 + 16 = 1870]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT RESIDENCE:  54 Middle Street, or  "Middle n Pine" Street, Forestville (Bristol), CT as of 1891, 1893, 1895, 1896, and 1897.
2 CONT Two years after Franklin Ensign Terry was born (1845) his parents, Ralph Terry and Ruth Lewis (Hubbell) Terry moved to a little house on Preston Street, Forestville, CT 1847.. They moved back to the Terry Homstead in 1850 (Three y
2 CONC ears after Annia Maria (Terry) Chase was born (Preston Street, Forestville, Ct). Franklin  lived the rest of his life at 54 Middle Street (Terry Homestead) 
2 CONT "At one time Papa [Franklin] boared with her [Mrs. Barnes] parent in Forestville"
2 CONT
2 CONT COMMUNITY ACTIVITIES:
2 CONT He also sang in the N.E.O.P., holding the office of treasurer at the time of his death and was clerk of the 5th School District.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Franklin E. Terry, son of Ralph and Ruth Terry, married Isabella J. Perry, who was also a descendant of Governor John Webster, and of Governor William Bradford.  This gives their descendants three lines back to Governor Bradford 
2 CONC and two lines to Governor Webster.  Noah Webster who wrote the famous dictionary was Mrs. Franklin E. Terry's great uncle."
2 CONT
2 CONT PICTURES:
