2 CONT Print of a picture taken of the first office of The FLetcher-Terry Company loacted in the barn where the company was started in 1903 by Fred Stanton Fletcher (B-9/16/1872, D-1/28/1963) and his father-in-law, Franklin Ensign Terry 
2 CONC (B-7/7/1845, D-7/7/1926).  The barn with the office and plant is located on the Terry Homstead, 54 Middle Street, Bristol, CT.  Copy made by Fred's grandson, Terry Bradley Fletcher in January, 1998.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Bristol Press, Bristol, CT, Thursday, July 8, 1926
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary of  Franklin Ensign Terry
2 CONT
2 CONT Franklin Ensign Terry passed away at his home on Middle St. Wednesday afternoon, July 7th.  The day being the occasion of his eighty-first birthday.  He had not enjoyed his usual good health for the past year and since Oct. had no
2 CONC t been able to go about as usual.  Since the first week in May he had been confined to his bed.  He had always been active in mind and body, taking a keen interest in affairs and in his home which he loved so well.  Only a short t
2 CONC ime ago he directed the planting of flowers in his garden, which had been a wealth of color for a number of years past.  He was wonderfully young in spirit, always having a quiet, cheerful way so that people did not realize his ad
2 CONC vanced years.  Of sound judgment, he was ever ready to  help anyone who came for his advice.  He was an example to all about him in patience and cheerfulness from the beginning of his illness until the end.
2 CONT The funeral services will be held at his late home Friday afternoon at half past two.  The burial will take place in Lake Avenue Cemetery.
2 CONT Mr. Terry was born in Bristol, in the old homestead in which he spent his life, on July 7, 1845, son of Ralph Ensign Terry and Ruth Lewis Hubbell.  His entire life was spent in Bristol, he being the fourth generation to live at th
2 CONC e homestead.  On Aug. 2nd, 1870 he was married to Isabella  J. Perry, of Waterbury who died April 15, 1919.  Both Mr. and Mrs. Terry came of staunch New England ancestry.  Mr. Terry was a lineal descendant of Josiah Lewis one of t
2 CONC he early settlers of Bristol.  He was a great nephew of Eli Terry, a pioneer clock maker who was inventor of the shelf clock in Conn.  Mrs. Terry was a great niece of Noah Webster of dictionary fame.
2 CONT Mr. Terry was employed by the E.N. Welch Co. in Forestville for many years and had charge of the movement department of the Sessions Co. for some time.  Later he went in to business with his son-in-law Fred S. Fletcher, and engage
2 CONC d in the manufacture of glass cutters and light hard-ware under the firm name of The Fletcher-Terry Co., Inc., he being president of the concern.  He was a member of the First Congregational Chuch of Bristol, of which church his f
2 CONC ather and grandfather, Samuel Terry, were prominent members.  He was very fond of music and sang in the N.E.O.P., holding the office of treasurer at the time of his death.  He was also clerk of School District Five.
2 CONT Mr. Terry is survived by two daughters, Mrs. Fred S. Fletcher and Miss Josephine Franklyn Terry; a brother Emerson Gillette Terry of Forestville; two grandsons, Arthur Terry Fletcher and Franklin Joseph Fletcher; and a great grand
2 CONC daughter Margery Fletcher, aged three years."
2 CONT
2 CONT Following are excerpts from reminiscences written in 1934 by Isabelle Webster (Terry) Fletcher (1878-1971) daughter of Franklin Ensign Terry.
2 CONT Franklin Ensign Terry was born 1845 in this house (the Samuel Terry homestead).  In two years his parents moved ot the little house on Preston Street, Forestville (where the Prestons lived when I was a girl.  Auntie Anna was born 
2 CONC there and when she was three years old they moved back to this house again, where Papa lived until he died in 1926 on his birthday July 7.  They always said he was a tall and slim lad, at times almost delicate looking, but I never
2 CONC  heard that he had any serious illness...
2 CONT My great grandfather (Samuel Terry) bought this house in 1828, and they made some alterations just what I do not know as the Botsfords changed it some too.  I think he tore out the big chimney and did away with the old fireplaces 
2 CONC which were in each front room and in the old long dining room.  The chimney went up in the front hall.  After this was taken out the chimney only came up in to the garret and stove pipes had to go through the bedrooms to the attic
2 CONC  chimney.  This had some advantages as they warmed the rooms they passed through.
2 CONT Samuel Terry was a brother of Eli Terry the clock maker.  He worked with his brother at one time and later made clocks in his own factory also.  At one time he made clocks down here near the river in what we called the old red sho
2 CONC p (although I never saw the original building which burned down before I was born).  He made the first clock in the Congregational Church tower.  Grandpa Terry (Ralph) [was] also down in what was once the old Andrews shop near the
2 CONC  river just off Preston St., Forestville.  At one time Samuel Terry's children lived around here in various houses.  Samuel Terry Jr. lived in the Beach Lumber Company's house - Edward in Daly's - John in the Hume place - Theodore
2 CONC  in the old Mitchel place with the pillars and one over on Pine St. where the Garey's lived...
2 CONT Papa worked in the old Welch Clock shop which stood near the railroad tracks near the Forestville station.  He turned pinions for a long while and thoroughly understood a clock movement.  Mr. Henry Bancroft and also his father...
2 CONT At the time I was married June 2, 1897 Papa was working in Winsted, [sic] Connecticut in the clock shop.  His father had worked there years before and had been foreman of the movements department.  Emerson (Terry) also worked ther
2 CONC e at the same time as his father at the age of sixteen.  Papa had been out of work here in Forestville for a long time.  There was a great depression...
2 CONT Grandpa Terry could always fix most anything that got out of order and also make new things and he always had the tools to do it.  If he did not have them he could make them.  There have been many tools here in the place that were
2 CONC  homemade.  Papa was the same way, he could do almost anything and was very particular...
2 CONT Papa and Mamma were married Aug. 2, 1870 eight years later Jan. 6, 1878 I came to town...
2 CONT In 1893 March Fred [Fletcher, Isabella's future husband] came East stopping at the World's Fair Grounds.  The Fair had not  yet opened.  He came to work for his Uncle Samuel Monce who owned  a business conducted in the old shop wh
2 CONC ich stood near the river where the Bell shop formerly stood.  The Bell shop was dismantled last year (1933).  He was  twenty years old...It was not until 1895 or so that I began to keep company with Fred...After graduating I becam
2 CONC e formally engaged and after a year I was married, June 2, 1897, here at home in the South Parlor, the same room in which my Great Grandmother Hubbell had been married...
2 CONT The factory [old shop near the river noted above] had burned down in Bristol and his uncle had moved the business to his own factory in Unionville.  We lived in Unionville three years.  Arthur was born there on April 15, 1898 in t
2 CONC he house on Farmington Ave. owned by Mr. Freeborn Alger, I have pleasant recollections of my home there and I made many friends...
2 CONT In 1900 Mamma was hurt by our horse and that year I came back to Bristol to take care of her eventually we moved here for Fred was dissatisfied in the way his uncle treated him in the business.  He was only getting two dollars a d
2 CONC ay when we were married and he had only one raise after that of twenty-five cents.
2 CONT Fred had been inventing a new glass cutter with interchangeable wheels.  This he offered to his Uncle but got no encouragement in regard to his adopting it.  Fred wanted to go into business for himself and after coming back home t
2 CONC o live Papa got Fred a position in the clock shop in Forestville.  In the fall of 1901 and into 1901 Fred started a business in the upper barn here on the place.  He worked nights here and held his place at the clock shop.  Then h
2 CONC e had to get someone to work here all the time.  These were bad years for him and he had to work hard chores and all made him busy.
2 CONT Papa was working at the Clock Shop too and it was about this time or a little later that Mr. Sessions gave Papa a good position.  Superintendent of the Movement department.  At last Papa was working so many years at the bench (all
2 CONC  at clock work) had a good position and one he was perfectly capable of filling efficiently.  He thoroughly understood clock movements both how to make them and making them run.  He could always fix a clock.
2 CONT He held his position until several years later when he resigned and gave all his time to our own business which had been organized and run under the name "The Fletcher-Terry Co."  It was a long hard pull in those years and Fred ha
2 CONC d to work very hard.
2 CONT
2 CONT LAST WILL AND TESTAMENT OF FRANKLIN E. TERRY
2 CONT Know All Men Herby That I, Franklin E. Terry, of the town of Bristol, county of Hartford and State of Connecticut, being now of sound and disposing mind and memor, do make and ordain this my last will and testament.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.  After payment of my just debts, funeral and probate expenses, I give and bequeath to my daughter, Josephine F. Terry, my piano, my stove, and my claw-leg table; and to my daughter Isabel T. Fletcher, my melodeon.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.  I give and bequeath to my grandson Arthur T. Fletcher the principal sum of his note to me for Two Hundred Dollars, on condition that he pays to my executor the amount of interest due and payable at the date of my death.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.  I give and bequeath to my said daughter Isabel a one-third interest in and to a note of Fifteen Hundred Dollars signed by The Fletcher-Terry Company, and payable to me; and I give and bequeath to my said daughter Josephine the
2 CONC  remaining two-thirds interest in said note.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.  I give and devise to my said daughter Isabel that piece of real estate located in Bristol, which is particularly described as follows:  Commencing at the northeast corned of my property located on Middle Street and extending w
2 CONC est two hundred eighty 9280) feet, thence south one hundred eighty feet (180), thence east one hundred fifty (150) feet, thence north thirty five (35) feet, thence east one hundred thirty (130) feet, thence north along Middle Stre
2 CONC et one hundred forty-five (145) feet to the starting point.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.  I give and devise to my said daughter Josephine that piece of real esate located in Bristol, which is particularly described as follows:  Commencing at the southeast corner of my property and extending two hundred seventy (270
2 CONC ) feet west along Mountain Road, to the so-called shop line, thence extending along said shop line to a point five (5) feet south from the extended line of the barn standing on my land known as the Homestead Plot, thence east to M
2 CONC iddle Street, and thence south along Middle Street one hundred ninety-one feet to the starting point.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.  All the rest and residue of my estate, real and personal, of whatever the same may consist or wherever it may be located, I give, devise, and bequeath to my said daughters, Josephine and Isabel, and to their heirs forever, to 
2 CONC be divided equally between them.  But I direct that any inheritance or transfer tax payable by my executor shall be paid out of the residue of my estate, so that all devises or bequests except the residuary gift shall be free of i
2 CONC nheritance tax.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.  I nominate and appoint the Bristol Trust Company, a corporation located in said town of Bristol, to be executor hereof.
2 CONT
2 CONT Franklin E. Terry signed and sealed his will on June 1, 1926.  The witnesses were George F. Thomas, Charles N. Gordon, and Epaphroditus Peck.  
2 CONT
2 CONT INVENTORY OF THE ESTATE OF FRANKLIN E. TERRY
2 CONT To the Court of Probate in and for the District of Bristol, County of Hartford and State of Connecticut:
2 CONT
2 CONT Estate of Franklin E. Terry, late of Bristol, in said District, deceased.  Died July 7, 1926.
2 CONT
2 CONT Real Estate
2 CONT 1.  A certain piece of real esate in the Town of Bristol, County of Hartford, and State of Connecticut, bounded as follows:
2 CONT Northerly:  By other land of the heirs of Franklin E. Terry;  Easterly:  By Middle Street;  Southerly:  By South Street, and Westerly:  By land of The Fletcher Terry Company.
