2 CONT Continued from Isabelle  Webster (Terry) Fletcher's notes dated 1934 profile:
2 CONT
2 CONT ...".There were two large cherry trees out North & I used to climb clear to the tip top branches above me that there were no branches above me & Mrs. Daly said it would worry her until I got back to earth again.  Loretta Kane used
2 CONC  to climb with me.
2 CONT
2 CONT When I was a little girl there were wonderful big chestnut trees growing on the mountain & we had lots of nuts but the blight killed all the trees & now a chestnut is a curiosity.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was one great tree over by the South line fence in the upper pasture.  It was so large that even the limbs were like big trees & Oh what big nuts grew on it.  The stump of the big tree which stood near where the bungalow sta
2 CONC ns is still there.  The nuts used to fall down onto the roof of the tent which we had there summers.  How we all liked to go nutting.  Even the old horse & cow used to hunt for them.  I have had Jimmie the horse follow me all arou
2 CONC nd in hopes that I would find some & give them to him.  Walnuts were pentiful too & our tree out here on the ledge was a fine one.  The nuts were so smooth & nice.  That tree very nearly died but for the last few years has been re
2 CONC viving somewhat.
2 CONT
2 CONT Papa had a fine apple orchard over across the mountain road & some seasons packed many barrels of apples & sold them.  Those trees are greatly damaged & some are gine entirely.  What few apples are there are stolen b the foreigner
2 CONC s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Years ago we were able to raise our winters supply of potatoes and they were nice ones too.  Now blight, scab & various things make it impossible to get good potatoes.  We have always had fine sweet corn through years ago.
2 CONT
2 CONT Papa raied a black corn called Black Mexican.  This was very  sweet & good eating.  Later the Yellow Bantam came into existance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Papa bought different kinds of strawberry plants, and these became crosed until he was able to raise a very fine berry which is mild & nice.  Dr. Kent in Forestville said they were the only strawberry he could eat & came up & got 
2 CONC quite a number of plants.  I do not know exactly all the names of the original strains but one was Philadelphia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of the shrubs & flowers are from the original plants which my Grandma Terry had in her garden.  The same flowering grince is still standing in the same spot where she had it.  Some years it is a blaze of beauty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Auntie always loved flowers & had her garden out North of the house.  Partly in the same place where her mother had had garden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandma Terry had a lovely circuar garden out South of the woodshed which I have always wanted to see restored.  She was a lover of flowers & always raised them.  Papa told of how she used to bake about fourteen pies in the big ov
2 CONC en out in the kitchen.  Just imagine fourteen pies at one time.  The old oven had not been torn down at that time.  They used the fireplace too & now that is closed up.
2 CONT
2 CONT Papa & Mamma were married Aug 2, 1870 eight years later Jan. 6, 1878 I came to town.  They were glad, I heard, to have a little one of their own although Ella Perry & Georgie Peck were living here at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT Auntie always said everyone was delighted & that I was a wonderful baby with lovely light curls all over my head.  There was a great time naming me & I was called several names before they finally steeled on Isabelle Webster & I w
2 CONC as baptised when I was a big girl of 12 or 13 pyears old.  I was such a white baby not the usual red color that they called me little Pearl latter on it was Pearlie.  They also called me Bessie Lulu.  Blad they didn't keep that an
2 CONC yway.  I have been Pearlie to my relatives & intimate friends.  Isabelle to many of my school mates & Belle to my husband & his relatives.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wellk I grew & was loved & cared for by dear parents & grandparents & Aunt Anna.  She loved me very dearly & I have wonderful memories of delightful things she did for me & gave me.  She was always ready to do things for me & I ha
2 CONC ve tried to have her in my mind when I have done for my own grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT As I became older & was funning around I had this dear home to roam over.  Grandpa Terry & Aunt Anna & Emerson lived on the North side of the house where I do now.  If I wanted a cookie Aunti's jar was always full & I was welcome 
2 CONC to have them. If I wanted candy Emerson brought me chocolate drops & hid them in his pockets.  Grandpa told me stories & sand a funnly little song to me while I sat in his lap.  Then I would follow him all ofer the place wherever 
2 CONC he was working.  He was always kind & loving & I loved to be with him.  He was not as tall as Papa but I have heard that he always dressed well & wore a high silk hat to church & drove nice horses & a two seated carriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT One birthday Auntie gave me a little red chair & she never got over the way I acted whcn she gave it to me.  It was one of her favorite stories.  How I loved that chair & yanked it all over the place.  She said many times she woul
2 CONC d look out in the garden & Grandpa would be working away & I would be somewhere near sitting in my little chair. (I have it yet).
2 CONT
2 CONT I can remember when I had a little square shawl & I used to wear it around my shoulders.  It was folded cornerwise & pinned in front.
2 CONT
2 CONT One time I seemed to rebel against work.  I wasn't very big & Mamma wanted me to come in & do something.  I stomped my foot & said "Make me work".  Mamma always had pretty things for me to wear & I liked them too.  I remember one 
2 CONC all over lace dress & hat to match, it was so pretty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aunt Ella loved me too. Tjeh said but I was only 4 years old when she died.  Mamma never got over her loss.  She must have been very lovely.  She married Uncle Ed Peck & lived in New York.  Georgie Peck was her son & Aunt Anna alw
2 CONC ays said he was so sweet & cunning when he was a little boy running around here.  He was always up to great capers too.  One time he put Ella on Papa's sulky & ran down the hill with it as he turned out onto the lawn back of the h
2 CONC ouse he ran under the clothesline & it took Ella right under the chin & tumbled her off over backward onto the ground.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Ella was here she took lessons of Miss Atkins (Bella Barnes) on the piano.  I always used to say I wanted to take lessons when I got big.  Too bad I never did.  I always had a great longing to sit down & pplan & be a big sing
2 CONC er.  Ella became a beautiful pianist & might have gone far if she had followed a career for she had a wonderful touch.  How Auntie loved to hear her play & Mamma too.  Ella always loved my folks very dearly & I loved her father Un
2 CONC cle Frank next to Papa.  He used to call me "Golden Hair" & he would write dear letters to me & bring me lovely gifts.  He was very deal to us all & it was a terryble blow when he died.  He was only 47 years old & Ella had just be
2 CONC en married to Frank Fairbanks.  It changed her life entirely.  
2 CONT
2 CONT My first teacher over here in this school was Mrs. Murrey whom I loved very dearly.  She had long dark brown curls tied up in a bunch high on her head in back & they fell to her shoulders.  She thought a great deal of me & I have 
2 CONC a lovely letter she wrote to me when I was visiting in Phila one winter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Miss Mary A. Callen taught in the upper room at the time (there were only two rooms) & after getting into the higher grades I went to her.  She was a fine woman in many weays & I often wonder what some of the modern teachers would
2 CONC  do if they suddenly had some of the discomforts to put up with which she did.  She lived in Plainville & took the train to Forestville early in the morning & many is the time she walked up to school from there & built a fire even
2 CONC  after she got here for the old woman who was tending the school did not always get it done.  I have her to thank for my love & understanding of maps which has stayed by me on my various trips.History too was a favorite & Reading.
2 CONC   She was a good reader & was considered very bright in Arithmetic.  She had many pupils to tire her & she was sometimes rather excitable & her punishments were pretty severe.  Though she never whipped me (perhaps I needed it some
2 CONC times though I always tried to get my lessons even though I could see funny things sometimes).  I did not graduate from this school however for Mamma wanted to send me to Bristol to schoo so I went to the South Side for over a yea
2 CONC r & graduated from there.  My teachers were Mr. Williams & Miss Olive Wellington.  I was very fond of them both & steadily improved my marks during the year.  Being a larger school & one of the central districts I had more advanta
2 CONC ges there than I had breen able to have over here.  There was a difference in the way the school was conducted & I found great pleasure & benefit in the change.  I took honorable mention from the Board of Education when I graduate
2 CONC d in 1892.  At that time they gave first & second prizes to the best scholars & a few honorable mentions.  Harriet Hutchinson (Mrs. Edgerton) took first prize that year & she also took first prize when four years later she graduat
2 CONC ed from the High School."
2 CONT
2 CONT (Written by Isabelle Webster (Terry) Fletcher 1934
2 CONT Continued in Josephine Franklyn Terry profile)
