1 NAME Chloe Elizabeth /Marr/
2 GIVN Chloe Elizabeth
2 SURN Marr
2 _MARNM Marr
1 NAME Chloe E. Marr
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 SEP 1832
2 PLAC Percy Ontario Canada
2 ADDR
3 CITY Percy
3 STAE Ontario
1 DEAT
2 DATE 12 OCT 1897
2 ADDR
3 CITY La Crosse
3 STAE WI
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Chloe Marr is listed in some of Leila H. Oberto (Valentine)s Pedigree Forms as Chloe anne Marr (same birth & death date).  Also listed on the internet site as Chloe Elizabeth Marr Randall, married Oct. 12, 1856 in 
2 CONC La Crosse, Wis.  Born Sept. 24, 1833 in Canada and died Oct. 12, 1897 in La Crosse, Wis.
2 CONT
2 CONT OBITUARY from THE WEEKLY ARGUS Newspaper of La Crosse, Wisconsin, dated October 16, 1897.  This partial very old newspaper is in with the geneology papers of Leila Oberto - now in the possession of Leila's daughter-in-law, Judy.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. John Valentine died at her home 1733 Kane St. Oct. 12, 1897 at 12:45 a.m. at the age of 65 years and 20 days.  Chloe Ann Marr was born Sept. 22,1832 at Percy, Canada and came to La Crosse in 1856. At this time  La Crosse was 
2 CONC a small city, the Milwaukee Road had just come into the city, not having a depot, there was no bridge crossing La Crosse River and she was obliged to cross it on what was known then as an oar stern with W. R. Sill assisting her.  
2 CONC Soon after she became the wife of John Valentine a member of the firm of Shepherd & Valentine who conducted an extensive lumbering business having a mill in North La Crosse being the main part of what  known as the old Davidson mi
2 CONC ll at the foot of St. James St. and one at Black River Falls.
2 CONT
2 CONT She had four children born to her, Flavius, Ellis, Lulu and Bryon, all survive her except Ellis who died 10 years ago. The old Valentine homestead, perhaps yet, lives in the minds of those who were here at the time which was locat
2 CONC ed on the corner of Mill and Wall Sts. The old house was framed at Black River Falls, brought down on a string of logs and set up on its location where it stood for at least 30 years, until the Burlington Road came in and purchase
2 CONC d the location for a depot, moved the main part to Grand Crossing which is now used for a paint
2 CONT
2 CONT shop. The smaller part is now located on wood St. in block 1500 and is owned n or occupied b.y Mr. Fradine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Valentine has been an invalid practically her natural life. For the last 23 years has been solely afflicted with eczema in its worst form, together with a broken hip caused by slipping on the ice this happened about 14 years 
2 CONC I, ago. Now her suffering is over. She tried to bear it all in a Christian spirit, and many a prayer has she offered that she might be called home and be releaved of her life long suffering and be at rest. Her friends and loved on
2 CONC es will miss her but rejoice when they know that her traveled stained garments are all laid down, puts on the robe of immortality.
