0 @N6000000008799867060@ NOTE From Ellis H. Dill  dill@email.rci.rutgers.edu
1 CONT
1 CONT THE WIVES OF IRA ELLIS (1802-1878)
1 CONT
1 CONT Ira Ellis was born on 25 Mar 1802 in Surry County, NC, a son of Etheldred Ellis and Elizabeth
1 CONT McKnight.  He migrated to Lincoln County, Tennessee, before 1830, and moved on to Newton County,
1 CONT Missouri, in 1937.  He had 11 children by several wives.  Because of the paucity of records on the
1 CONT frontier, the identity of his wives is not fully recorded, and some questions persist as to the
1 CONT number and names of his wives.  Based on the evidence cited below, the most likely scenario
1 CONT seems to be to be the following one.
1 CONT
1 CONT Name             birth date  marriage  death  children
1 CONT
1 CONT Mary BLEDSOE         ?         c1828  c1842       Thomas (1830)
1 CONT Asbury (1832)
1 CONT John   (1835)
1 CONT Sarah  (1836)
1 CONT William(1839)
1 CONT Lafe   (1841)
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth DUNAGAN   c1819      c1842  c1851       Ira (1843)
1 CONT James  (1844)
1 CONT George (1845)
1 CONT
1 CONT Nancy               1822       c1851  c1852       none
1 CONT the widow MOORE
1 CONT
1 CONT Nancy Manerva FORD  1821/1822  c1852  8 Nov 1861  Martha (1853)
1 CONT Bascom (1859)
1 CONT
1 CONT Adaline YOUNG       1822        1864  1889        none
1 CONT
1 CONT We must, however, keep an open mind and wait for more evidence.  It is possible, with
1 CONT adjustment of the above dates, that there were other wives or that the two of name Nancy were
1 CONT one.
0 @N6000000000507097839@ NOTE From: "Diana Betancourt & Richard A. Marconi" <dbmram@bellsouth.net>
1 CONT To: "Jesse McWhirter" <jomac@vallnet.com>
1 CONT Subject: Re: [TNSUMNER] Bate's 2nd TN Inf Regiment
1 CONT Date: Tuesday, February 05, 2002 12:53 PM
1 CONT
1 CONT Hi Jesse, James T.C. McKnight was the son of David McKnight and Martha
1 CONT (Patsy) McWhirter. James was a merchant in Murfreesboro when he enlisted on
1 CONT April 27, 1861 in Murfreesboro in Captain S.N White's company, later Company
1 CONT A, 2nd TN.
1 CONT At the time of his enlistment, he wrote out his will and distributed his
1 CONT property. He was a widower having married in 1846 to Mary E. Jetton. Mary
1 CONT died in 1849, they had no children. After the company was organized it went
1 CONT to Nashville where it was organized in the 2nd TN.  James was a private at
1 CONT that time. From there he went to Lynchburg, VA and was sworn into
1 CONT Confederate service. James served at Aquia Creek Batteries, VA with the
1 CONT regiment. He reenlisted with it in early 1862 and was given a 60 day
1 CONT furlough. When A.S. Johnston was organizing forces at Corinth, a call went
1 CONT out the 2nd TN to meet there. Apparently James made it there and
1 CONT participated in the Battle of Shiloh on 6-7 April 1862. On May 1, 1862 he
1 CONT was elected Captain and commander of Company A. He participated in the
1 CONT battles of 1st Manassas, Shiloh, Richmond, KY, Perryville, KY, Murfreesboro
1 CONT and Chickamauga. At Richmond, KY, James was wounded in the knee. Then on the
1 CONT second day of the Battle of Chickamauga, 19-20 Sep 1863, he was killed in
1 CONT action while leading his troops in Cleburne's morning engagement. I have not
1 CONT yet found where is buried. He is mentioned in BG L.E. Polk's report of the
1 CONT battle and is listed on the Confederate Roll of Honor for the Battle of
1 CONT Chickamauga. You can find these in the Official Record of the War of the
1 CONT Rebellion, series I, vol. XXX, part II.
1 CONT As far as I know, he was the only one of the family that stayed in
1 CONT Tennessee.  His brothers, George D.A. McKnight (my g-g-grandfather), Samuel
1 CONT H.A. McKnight, their sister and brother in law, John M. Andrews, all moved
1 CONT to Ferris, Ellis County, Texas in 1850. Samuel and John served in cavalry
1 CONT units from Texas during the war.
1 CONT I hope this helps.
1 CONT
1 CONT Take care
1 CONT Richard Marconi
1 CONT ----- Original Message -----
1 CONT From: "Jesse McWhirter" <jomac@vallnet.com>
1 CONT To: <dbmram@bellsouth.net>
1 CONT Sent: Tuesday, February 05, 2002 12:41 PM
1 CONT Subject: Re: [TNSUMNER] Bate's 2nd TN Inf Regiment
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT >
1 CONT > ----- Original Message -----
1 CONT > From: <dbmram@bellsouth.net>
1 CONT > To: <TNSUMNER-L@rootsweb.com>
1 CONT > Sent: Monday, February 04, 2002 11:59 AM
1 CONT > Subject: [TNSUMNER] Bate's 2nd TN Inf Regiment
1 CONT >
1 CONT >
1 CONT > > This is a Message Board Post that is gatewayed to this mailing list.
1 CONT > >
1 CONT > > Classification: Query
1 CONT > >
1 CONT > > Message Board URL:
1 CONT > >
1 CONT > > http://boards.ancestry.com/mbexec/msg/rw/HiB.2ACI/2151
1 CONT > >
1 CONT > > Message Board Post:
1 CONT > >
1 CONT > > I am researching the history of Bate's 2nd TN Inf Regt for my masters
1 CONT > thesis. If anyone has information/stories about the men and the unit I
1 CONT would
1 CONT > greatly appreciate it if you would be willing to share that information
1 CONT with
1 CONT > me. I am interested in the regiment because my great-great grand uncle,
1 CONT > Capt. James T.C. McKnight was commander of Company A, 2nd TN and was
1 CONT killed
1 CONT > 20 Sep 63 at Chickamauga. Thank you
1 CONT > > I would like to exchange information on James McKnight.
1 CONT > >
1 CONT >
0 @N6000000008307981566@ NOTE "Leading Citizens of Franklin County" indicates that George was a native of
1 CONT Deerfield, a man of many talents.  Lived in Williamsburg and in Colraine.
1 CONT (Children's middle names and 2nd wife's provided by Harriett Allen)
1 CONT
1 CONT George W. Smith was a man of many resources, being a blacksmith, butcher, and farmer.  He lived in Williamsburg and in Colrain, and died in Little Falls, N.Y., in 1890; his wife passed away in 1866.  
1 CONT
1 CONT Intention of marriage, Nov 24 1842, in Ashfield Town Records vol #6,, Pg 7
1 CONT and Nov 22 1842 in Conway Town Records vol misc 1829-51, pg 82, no 9.
1 CONT
1 CONT Evening Times, January 22, 1890 
1 CONT Little Falls, Montgomery County, New York,
1 CONT "After a brief illness from typhoid pneumonia, George W. Smith, father of Mrs Squire Bailey of John street died at Mr. Bailey’s residence this morning, aged 72 years.  Mr. Smith came here on a visit to his daughters several weeks 
1 CONC ago.   Thus a family reunion has been rendered an occasion of profound sorrow.  The remains will be taken to Bennington, Vt., the home of the deceased, to-marrow and the funeral will occur Friday.  Internment will be at [A]shfield
1 CONC , Mass." 
1 CONT
1 CONT 1900 New York census indicates Squire Bailey's wife is Jennie.  Jennie was born March 1860 in MA as were her parents.  In 1900 they had been married 10 years.  Is it possible George was visiting the 'newly weds' at the time of his
1 CONC  death?
0 @N6000000008307391732@ NOTE m. 10-01-1851 Lucinda Cranston
0 @N6000000006348736696@ NOTE Before Mr. Cooley left, a serious effort had been made to erect a new church by the appointment of a committee, among whom were Mr. Morse [James Otis]  and Oliver Judd, the latter the head of an ingenious family who came from Conn
1 CONC ecticut, and established themselves in the manufacture of iron, and all of whom being musical, long sustained the efficiency of the service of song. Edwin Judd, who might have been called, like Aristides, the just, bore the charac
1 CONC ter of a Nestor to the village, and sang in the choir for forty years, scarcely missing a Sunday.
